-weights-v1\",\n      \"liveWeightsFrozen\": true,\n      \"recommendations\": [\n        {\n          \"skillId\": \"sk-code\",\n          \"liveScore\": 0.17515,\n          \"shadowScore\": 0.161,\n          \"delta\": -0.01415,\n          \"dominantShadowLane\": \"lexical\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"skillId\": \"sk-doc\",\n          \"liveScore\": 0.172207,\n          \"shadowScore\": 0.15828,\n          \"delta\": -0.013927,\n          \"dominantShadowLane\": \"lexical\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"skillId\": \"system-spec-kit\",\n          \"liveScore\": 0.089693,\n          \"shadowScore\": 0.0937,\n          \"delta\": 0.004007,\n          \"dominantShadowLane\": \"lexical\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\\\"prompt\\\":\\\"Add a failing test then change the pointer detail wording in the agent instructions pointer check\\\"}' --format json\"}"},"time":{"start":1794558766003,"end":1794558766714}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794558769536,"sessionID":"ses_e2bf6bc5bffePiQWy1Q3jiC9P1","part":{"id":"prt_1d409b980001QN3yGUq7ZPkrND","sessionID":"ses_e2bf6bc5bffePiQWy1Q3jiC9P1","messageID":"msg_1d4098064001UQ8Q5aTtUSjK0V","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"22c05ab60ee0cf63191b3ff56a4779249266534f","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":3600,"output":163,"reasoning":624,"cache":{"read":18048,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794558774056,"sessionID":"ses_e2bf6bc5bffePiQWy1Q3jiC9P1","part":{"id":"prt_1d409cb28001KD8qhRW26Le2MP","sessionID":"ses_e2bf6bc5bffePiQWy1Q3jiC9P1","messageID":"msg_1d409b985001HQ1kxYcJpDny6v","type":"step-start","snapshot":"0b2db90a5dd5eb63d1383f5665c7423e93488a41"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794558775171,"sessionID":"ses_e2bf6bc5bffePiQWy1Q3jiC9P1","part":{"partID":"prt_1d409cd7c002A9RYcbziLVl4sR","sessionID":"ses_e2bf6bc5bffePiQWy1Q3jiC9P1","messageID":"msg_1d409b985001HQ1kxYcJpDny6v","type":"tool","id":"call_e4de199ec942482f9d80ac67","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"REPO RULES.md"},"output":"Read file REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"REPO RULES.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794558774652,"end":1794558775171}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794558775175,"sessionID":"ses_e2bf6bc5bffePiQWy1Q3jiC9P1","part":{"partID":"prt_1d409ce3f001e5pF7z9Px7kWkj","sessionID":"ses_e2bf6bc5bffePiQWy1Q3jiC9P1","messageID":"msg_1d409b985001HQ1kxYcJpDny6v","type":"tool","id":"call_011ad6dd9f8147d0a724c0d6","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-check.cjs"},"output":"Read file .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-check.cjs, lines 1-1061\n1: #!/usr/bin/env node\n2: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n3: // MODULE: Shipped Instruction Check Engine\n4: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n5: 'use strict';\n6: \n7: // A repository that took the shipped instruction template can be in any of seven\n8: // states, and nothing in it says which one. Its shipped sections may be current, one\n9: // release behind, or edited in place; its own content may sit in the marked block or be\n10: // scattered between shipped sections; it may have no root document at all. Without that\n11: // answer there is nothing to plan a rewrite from, and no way to tell a safe replacement\n12: // from one that would drop a line somebody wrote. So this engine reports, per row and\n13: // without writing a byte, what the repository holds against the manifest.\n14: //\n15: // Three of the twelve rows are answers this engine must not invent. The delivery budget\n16: // and its anchors belong to the rule-copy canary, the Cursor pointer block belongs to\n17: // the gate 1 sync, and the router belongs to the repo-rules checker. Each already has\n18: // tests and an owner, so each is run as a child process and its own lines are carried\n19: // into the report; re-deriving any of them here would be a second opinion that drifts\n20: // from the first. The rest are pure classifiers over one read of the target tree.\n21: //\n22: // The run is read-only by construction: the only paths it opens for reading are under\n23: // the target root plus the manifest, and the three child tools it runs are read-only\n24: // modes. A repository that cannot be classified at all (no manifest, no target\n25: // directory, an unreadable root document) is a failed precondition and exits 2, because\n26: // an empty report would read like a clean one.\n27: //\n28: // Usage: agents-md-check.cjs [--root <dir>] [--manifest <path>] [--source] [--json]\n29: //   --root <dir>      classify this repository instead of the one this script ships in\n30: //   --manifest <path> read this manifest instead of the shipped one\n31: //   --source          run the template-purity row even outside the source repository\n32: //   --json            print one JSON object instead of the text report\n33: // Exit codes: 0 nothing to report, 1 at least one finding or an unproven row,\n34: //             2 a precondition failed\n35: \n36: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n37: // 1. IMPORTS\n38: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n39: \n40: const { spawnSync } = require('node:child_process');\n41: const fs = require('node:fs');\n42: const path = require('node:path');\n43: \n44: const {\n45:   classifyBody,\n46:   deliveryAnchorEnd,\n47:   matchSections,\n48:   segment,\n49: } = require('./lib/agents-md-sections.cjs');\n50: const { sourceStatus } = require('./agents-md-manifest.cjs');\n51: \n52: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n53: // 2. CONSTANTS\n54: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n55: \n56: const TAG = '[agents-md-check]';\n57: const REPO_ROOT = path.resolve(__dirname, '../../../..');\n58: const MANIFEST_PATH = path.resolve(__dirname, '../assets/agents-md-manifest.json');\n59: const GENERATOR = path.resolve(__dirname, 'agents-md-manifest.cjs');\n60: // The router checker ships beside the skill that authors rules, and the manifest links\n61: // it only through the router's own row, so its path is named here once.\n62: const REPO_RULES_CHECKER = path.resolve(\n63:   REPO_ROOT,\n64:   '.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-repo-rule/scripts/check-repo-rules.cjs',\n65: );\n66: // The checker's own tag prefix. Matching on it is what keeps this row a carrier of that\n67: // tool's verdict instead of a second implementation of its checks.\n68: const REPO_RULES_TAG = '[repo-rules-check]';\n69: const USAGE = 'Usage: node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-check.cjs [--root <dir>] [--manifest <path>] [--source] [--json]';\n70: const TOOL_TIMEOUT_MS = 120000;\n71: \n72: // A repository where fewer than half the shipped sections survive is carrying its own\n73: // document, not a copy of the template. Above the line the question becomes which parts\n74: // of the template are still there, which is a per-section answer.\n75: const FOREIGN_SHARE = 0.5;\n76: \n77: const PASS = 'PASS';\n78: const FINDING = 'FINDING';\n79: const UNKNOWN = 'UNKNOWN';\n80: const INFO = 'INFO';\n81: \n82: class UsageError extends Error {}\n83: \n84: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n85: // 3. ARGUMENTS\n86: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n87: \n88: function parseArgs(argv) {\n89:   const options = { root: REPO_ROOT, manifest: MANIFEST_PATH, source: false, json: false, help: false };\n90:   for (let index = 0; index < argv.length; index += 1) {\n91:     const argument = argv[index];\n92:     if (argument === '--root') {\n93:       const value = argv[index + 1];\n94:       if (!value || value.startsWith('--')) throw new UsageError('--root needs a directory');\n95:       options.root = path.resolve(value);\n96:       index += 1;\n97:     } else if (argument === '--manifest') {\n98:       const value = argv[index + 1];\n99:       if (!value || value.startsWith('--')) throw new UsageError('--manifest needs a file path');\n100:       options.manifest = path.resolve(value);\n101:       index += 1;\n102:     } else if (argument === '--source') {\n103:       options.source = true;\n104:     } else if (argument === '--json') {\n105:       options.json = true;\n106:     } else if (argument === '--help' || argument === '-h') {\n107:       options.help = true;\n108:     } else {\n109:       throw new UsageError(`unknown argument: ${argument}`);\n110:     }\n111:   }\n112:   return options;\n113: }\n114: \n115: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n116: // 4. READING THE TARGET ONCE\n117: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n118: \n119: // Section offsets are byte offsets because the delivery budget is a byte count, while a\n120: // reader opens the file by line number. One conversion, in one place.\n121: function lineNumberAt(text, byteOffset) {\n122:   return Buffer.from(text, 'utf8').subarray(0, byteOffset).toString('utf8').split('\\n').length;\n123: }\n124: \n125: function trimmedLines(text) {\n126:   const lines = new Set();\n127:   for (const raw of String(text).split('\\n')) {\n128:     const line = raw.trim();\n129:     if (line !== '' && line !== '---') lines.add(line);\n130:   }\n131:   return lines;\n132: }\n133: \n134: // The repository's own lines, in order and with repeats. A set would report one line for\n135: // two occurrences, and a replacement loses both, so a duplicate is a second line to keep.\n136: function localLinesOf(body, reference) {\n137:   const known = trimmedLines(reference);\n138:   const local = [];\n139:   for (const raw of body.split('\\n')) {\n140:     const line = raw.trim();\n141:     if (line === '' || line === '---' || known.has(line)) continue;\n142:     local.push(line);\n143:   }\n144:   return local;\n145: }\n146: \n147: function knownBodies(manifest) {\n148:   const sections = manifest.derived && manifest.derived.sections ? manifest.derived.sections : {};\n149:   return {\n150:     current: Object.keys(sections).map((id) => ({ id, sha256: sections[id].sha256, body: sections[id].body })),\n151:     history: (manifest.derived && manifest.derived.history) || [],\n152:   };\n153: }\n154: \n155: // The block carries a heading and a note that the template itself ships, so \"the block is\n156: // empty\" means nothing beyond those two lines, not that the markers bracket no text.\n157: function blockHasContent(block, markers) {\n158:   if (!block || !block.complete) return false;\n159:   const shipped = new Set([markers.heading.trim(), markers.note.trim()]);\n160:   for (const raw of block.content.split('\\n')) {\n161:     const line = raw.trim();\n162:     if (line === '' || shipped.has(line)) continue;\n163:     return true;\n164:   }\n165:   return false;\n166: }\n167: \n168: /**\n169:  * Read the target tree once and keep what every row needs.\n170:  *\n171:  * @param {{root: string, manifest: Object}} input\n172:  * @returns {Object|null} Null when the root directory is not there.\n173:  */\n174: function readTarget({ root, manifest }) {\n175:   if (!fs.existsSync(root) || !fs.statSync(root).isDirectory()) return null;\n176: \n177:   const rootPath = path.join(root, manifest.root_file);\n178:   const exists = fs.existsSync(rootPath);\n179:   const text = exists ? fs.readFileSync(rootPath, 'utf8') : null;\n180:   const segmentation = exists\n181:     ? segment(text, manifest.markers, manifest.sections)\n182:     : { sections: [], block: null, prelude: null };\n183:   const pairing = matchSections(segmentation.sections, manifest.sections, segmentation.prelude);\n184: \n185:   return {\n186:     root,\n187:     rootPath,\n188:     exists,\n189:     text,\n190:     totalBytes: exists ? Buffer.byteLength(text, 'utf8') : 0,\n191:     block: segmentation.block,\n192:     prelude: segmentation.prelude,\n193:     sections: segmentation.sections,\n194:     matched: pairing.matched,\n195:     unregistered: pairing.unregistered,\n196:     unmatched: pairing.unmatched,\n197:     conflicts: pairing.conflicts,\n198:     currency: pairing.matched.map((row) => ({\n199:       id: row.entry.id,\n200:       section: row.section,\n201:       ...classifyBody(row.section.body, knownBodies(manifest)),\n202:     })),\n203:     blockContent: blockHasContent(segmentation.block, manifest.markers),\n204:   };\n205: }\n206: \n207: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n208: // 5. THE TWELVE ROWS\n209: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n210: \n211: function row(id, name, state, detail, extra = {}) {\n212:   return { id, name, state, detail, lines: extra.lines || [], entries: extra.entries || [] };\n213: }\n214: \n215: // A1. The one row an operator reads first: which of the seven shapes this repository is.\n216: function rowInstallState(context, manifest) {\n217:   const claudePath = path.join(context.root, 'CLAUDE.md');\n218:   if (!context.exists) {\n219:     if (fs.existsSync(claudePath)) {\n220:       return row('A1', 'install state', FINDING, 'claude-only',\n221:         { lines: [`no ${manifest.root_file}; CLAUDE.md is the only instruction file`] });\n222:     }\n223:     return row('A1', 'install state', FINDING, 'absent',\n224:       { lines: [`no ${manifest.root_file} and no CLAUDE.md`] });\n225:   }\n226: \n227:   const share = manifest.sections.length === 0 ? 0 : context.matched.length / manifest.sections.length;\n228:   if (share < FOREIGN_SHARE) {\n229:     return row('A1', 'install state', FINDING, 'foreign', {\n230:       lines: [\n231:         `${context.matched.length} of ${manifest.sections.length} shipped sections match, under the ${Math.round(FOREIGN_SHARE * 100)}% floor`,\n232:         'the root document is the repository\\'s own file',\n233:       ],\n234:     });\n235:   }\n236: \n237:   const localEdits = context.currency.filter((entry) => entry.state === 'edited' || entry.state === 'unknown-base');\n238:   const behind = context.currency.filter((entry) => entry.state === 'stale-known');\n239:   // A local edit outranks a missed release in the reading order: it is the shape that\n240:   // loses text if somebody replaces the document, and staleness only loses a patch.\n241:   if (localEdits.length > 0 || context.unregistered.length > 0) {\n242:     const lines = [];\n243:     for (const entry of localEdits) lines.push(`${entry.id}: ${entry.state}`);\n244:     for (const entry of context.unregistered) {\n245:       lines.push(entry.heading === null\n246:         ? 'unmarked text before the first heading'\n247:         : `unmarked section ${JSON.stringify(entry.heading)}`);\n248:     }\n249:     return row('A1', 'install state', FINDING, 'forked', { lines });\n250:   }\n251:   if (behind.length > 0) {\n252:     return row('A1', 'install state', FINDING, 'stale',\n253:       { lines: behind.map((entry) => `${entry.id}: ${entry.state}`) });\n254:   }\n255:   if (context.blockContent) {\n256:     return row('A1', 'install state', PASS, 'adopted',\n257:       { lines: ['every shipped section is current and the repository block carries content'] });\n258:   }\n259:   return row('A1', 'install state', PASS, 'pure',\n260:     { lines: ['every shipped section is current and the repository block is empty'] });\n261: }\n262: \n263: // A2. Which shipped sections survived, by the prefix each authored entry carries.\n264: function rowSectionsPresent(context, manifest) {\n265:   const present = new Set(context.matched.map((row_) => row_.entry.id));\n266:   const missing = manifest.sections.map((entry) => entry.id).filter((id) => !present.has(id));\n267:   const detail = `${present.size}/${manifest.sections.length} matched`;\n268:   if (missing.length === 0 && context.conflicts.length === 0) {\n269:     return row('A2', 'sections present', PASS, detail);\n270:   }\n271:   const lines = missing.map((id) => `missing section ${id}`);\n272:   for (const conflict of context.conflicts) lines.push(conflict.message);\n273:   return row('A2', 'sections present', FINDING, `${present.size}/${manifest.sections.length} matched, ${missing.length} missing`, {\n274:     lines,\n275:     entries: missing.map((id) => ({ id, present: false })),\n276:   });\n277: }\n278: \n279: // The byte offset of the first line that is exactly this text, or -1 when the document\n280: // does not carry it.\n281: function firstLineOffset(text, needle) {\n282:   if (text.startsWith(needle)) return 0;\n283:   const at = text.indexOf(`\\n${needle}`);\n284:   return at === -1 ? -1 : at + 1;\n285: }\n286: \n287: // The shipped numbering, read from the pin that names it. Hundreds of files cite these\n288: // numbers, and the document carries its fourth section before its third, so the citation\n289: // order is not the reading order and only the pin carries the expected one.\n290: function numberingReport(context, manifest) {\n291:   if (!context.exists) return { state: INFO, detail: 'no root document to number', lines: [] };\n292: \n293:   const pin = (manifest.pins || []).find((entry) => entry.kind === 'order');\n294:   if (!pin) return { state: INFO, detail: 'the manifest declares no numbering pin', lines: [] };\n295: \n296:   const found = [];\n297:   const absent = [];\n298:   for (const text of pin.texts) {\n299:     const at = firstLineOffset(context.text, text);\n300:     if (at === -1) absent.push(text.trim());\n301:     else found.push({ text: text.trim(), at });\n302:   }\n303:   const expected = pin.texts.map((text) => text.trim()).filter((text) => found.some((entry) => entry.text === text));\n304:   const actual = found.slice().sort((left, right) => left.at - right.at).map((entry) => entry.text);\n305: \n306:   const lines = absent.map((text) => `missing section number ${JSON.stringify(text)}`);\n307:   if (expected.join('|') !== actual.join('|')) {\n308:     lines.push(`the numbers appear as ${actual.join(', ')} instead of ${expected.join(', ')}`);\n309:   }\n310:   return { state: lines.length === 0 ? PASS : FINDING, detail: `${found.length}/${pin.texts.length} section numbers in place`, lines };\n311: }\n312: \n313: // A3. Shipped sections in manifest order, and section numbers unchanged.\n314: function rowOrderAndNumbering(context, manifest) {\n315:   const order = manifest.sections.map((entry) => entry.id);\n316:   const seen = context.matched.map((entry) => entry.entry.id);\n317:   const expected = order.filter((id) => seen.includes(id));\n318:   const numbering = numberingReport(context, manifest);\n319: \n320:   const lines = [...numbering.lines];\n321:   if (expected.join('|') !== seen.join('|')) {\n322:     const first = seen.findIndex((id, index) => id !== expected[index]);\n323:     lines.unshift(`section order differs at position ${first + 1}: found ${seen[first]}, expected ${expected[first]}`);\n324:   }\n325:   const state = lines.length === 0 ? PASS : FINDING;\n326:   return row('A3', 'order and numbering', state, state === PASS ? 'manifest order and section numbers unchanged' : 'order or numbering drifted', { lines });\n327: }\n328: \n329: // A4. Per section, whether the body is the shipped one, an older release, a local edit\n330: // or something with no base to name.\n331: function rowCurrency(context) {\n332:   const counts = { current: 0, 'stale-known': 0, edited: 0, 'unknown-base': 0 };\n333:   const entries = [];\n334:   for (const entry of context.currency) {\n335:     counts[entry.state] += 1;\n336:     entries.push({ id: entry.id, state: entry.state, base: entry.base || null });\n337:   }\n338:   // Nothing to classify is not a clean bill: the rows around this one report the empty or\n339:   // foreign document, and a pass here would read as currency that was checked.\n340:   if (entries.length === 0) {\n341:     return row('A4', 'section currency', INFO, 'no shipped section matched, so there is no body to classify');\n342:   }\n343:   const detail = `${counts.current} current, ${counts['stale-known']} stale-known, ${counts.edited} edited, ${counts['unknown-base']} unknown-base`;\n344:   const lines = [];\n345:   for (const entry of context.currency) {\n346:     if (entry.state === 'current') continue;\n347:     const base = entry.base ? entry.base.slice(0, 12) : 'none';\n348:     lines.push(`${entry.id}: ${entry.state} (closest known body ${base})`);\n349:   }\n350:   return row('A4', 'section currency', lines.length === 0 ? PASS : FINDING, detail, { lines, entries });\n351: }\n352: \n353: // A5. Every string downstream tooling greps out of the document, checked by its rule.\n354: function rowPins(context, manifest) {\n355:   const entries = [];\n356:   const byId = new Map(context.matched.map((entry) => [entry.entry.id, entry.section]));\n357: \n358:   for (const pin of manifest.pins || []) {\n359:     const verdict = pinVerdict(pin, context, manifest, byId);\n360:     entries.push({ id: pin.id, kind: pin.kind, state: verdict.state, detail: verdict.detail });\n361:   }\n362: \n363:   const failed = entries.filter((entry) => entry.state === FINDING);\n364:   const unmeasured = entries.filter((entry) => entry.state === UNKNOWN);\n365:   const lines = failed.map((entry) => `${entry.id} (${entry.kind}): ${entry.detail}`);\n366:   for (const entry of unmeasured) lines.push(`${entry.id} (${entry.kind}): ${entry.detail}`);\n367:   const state = failed.length > 0 ? FINDING : (unmeasured.length > 0 ? UNKNOWN : PASS);\n368:   const detail = `${entries.length - failed.length - unmeasured.length}/${entries.length} pins hold${unmeasured.length > 0 ? `, ${unmeasured.length} unmeasured` : ''}`;\n369:   return row('A5', 'pins', state, detail, { lines, entries });\n370: }\n371: \n372: function pinVerdict(pin, context, manifest, byId) {\n373:   const section = pin.section ? byId.get(pin.section) : null;\n374:   if (pin.section && !section) {\n375:     return { state: FINDING, detail: `the section ${pin.section} that carries this pin is missing` };\n376:   }\n377: \n378:   switch (pin.kind) {\n379:     case 'exact': {\n380:       const missing = pin.texts.filter((text) => !section.body.includes(text));\n381:       return missing.length === 0\n382:         ? { state: PASS, detail: `all ${pin.texts.length} strings present in ${pin.section}` }\n383:         : { state: FINDING, detail: `missing ${JSON.stringify(missing[0])} in ${pin.section}` };\n384:     }\n385:     case 'line': {\n386:       const required = (pin.requires || pin.texts).map((text) => text.toLowerCase());\n387:       const hit = section.body.split('\\n').some((line) => {\n388:         const lower = line.toLowerCase();\n389:         return required.every((text) => lower.includes(text));\n390:       });\n391:       return hit\n392:         ? { state: PASS, detail: `a line in ${pin.section} carries ${required.join(', ')}` }\n393:         : { state: FINDING, detail: `no line in ${pin.section} carries ${required.join(', ')}` };\n394:     }\n395:     case 'first-occurrence': {\n396:       const at = context.text.indexOf(pin.texts[0]);\n397:       const inside = at >= section.start && at < section.end;\n398:       if (inside) return { state: PASS, detail: `the first occurrence sits inside ${pin.section}` };\n399:       return {\n400:         state: FINDING,\n401:         detail: at === -1\n402:           ? `the text ${JSON.stringify(pin.texts[0])} is absent`\n403:           : `the first occurrence at byte ${at} sits outside ${pin.section} (${section.start}..${section.end}), so anything reading the first line of the document follows it instead`,\n404:       };\n405:     }\n406:     case 'delivery-anchor': {\n407:       const end = deliveryAnchorEnd(section, pin);\n408:       if (end === null) return { state: FINDING, detail: `the anchor text is absent from ${pin.section}` };\n409:       const limit = manifest.budget.prefix_bytes;\n410:       return end <= limit\n411:         ? { state: PASS, detail: `${pin.section} ends at byte ${end}, inside ${limit}` }\n412:         : { state: FINDING, detail: `${pin.section} ends at byte ${end}, past the ${limit}-byte delivery prefix` };\n413:     }\n414:     case 'budget': {\n415:       const declared = manifest.budget[pin.limit_key];\n416:       const pinned = Number(pin.texts[0]);\n417:       return declared === pinned\n418:         ? { state: PASS, detail: `${pin.limit_key} is ${declared}` }\n419:         : { state: FINDING, detail: `${pin.limit_key} reads ${declared}, the pin says ${pinned}` };\n420:     }\n421:     case 'order': {\n422:       const numbering = numberingReport(context, manifest);\n423:       return { state: numbering.state, detail: numbering.detail };\n424:     }\n425:     case 'file': {\n426:       const target = path.join(context.root, pin.path);\n427:       return fs.existsSync(target)\n428:         ? { state: PASS, detail: `${pin.path} exists` }\n429:         : { state: FINDING, detail: `${pin.path} is missing` };\n430:     }\n431:     default:\n432:       // A kind the engine has no rule for is a pin nobody measured. Counting it as a hold\n433:       // would report a manifest edit as verified.\n434:       return { state: UNKNOWN, detail: `no rule for a pin of kind ${pin.kind}` };\n435:   }\n436: }\n437: \n438: // A6. The delivery budget: the canary's own AGENTS.md lines, plus the two measurements\n439: // the report needs. Only this row's file is kept, because the canary also pins the skill\n440: // it ships in and a consumer legitimately has none of those.\n441: function rowBudget(context, manifest, tools) {\n442:   if (typeof tools.canary !== 'string' || !fs.existsSync(tools.canary)) {\n443:     return row('A6', 'delivery budget', UNKNOWN,\n444:       `the rule-copy canary is not installed at ${tools.canary || '(no path)'}; the budget cannot be measured`);\n445:   }\n446: \n447:   const run = spawnSync(process.execPath, [tools.canary, '--root', context.root], {\n448:     encoding: 'utf8',\n449:     timeout: TOOL_TIMEOUT_MS,\n450:     maxBuffer: 16 * 1024 * 1024,\n451:   });\n452:   const output = `${run.stdout || ''}\\n${run.stderr || ''}`.split('\\n').map((line) => line.trim()).filter((line) => line !== '');\n453:   if (run.error || (run.status !== 0 && run.status !== 1)) {\n454:     return row('A6', 'delivery budget', UNKNOWN,\n455:       `the rule-copy canary could not run: ${run.error ? run.error.message : `exit ${run.status}`}`);\n456:   }\n457: \n458:   // The canary prints its verdict, an anchor table and its findings. Only the findings\n459:   // that name this document belong to this row; its PASS and table lines are its own\n460:   // report, and the anchor end byte is measured below instead of parsed back out.\n461:   const own = output\n462:     .filter((line) => line.includes(manifest.root_file))\n463:     .filter((line) => /^(MISSING|BLOCKED):/u.test(line));\n464:   const elsewhere = output.filter((line) => !line.includes(manifest.root_file));\n465: \n466:   const limit = manifest.budget.prefix_bytes;\n467:   const ceiling = manifest.budget.max_bytes;\n468:   const prefixEnd = (manifest.pins || [])\n469:     .filter((pin) => pin.kind === 'delivery-anchor')\n470:     .reduce((max, pin) => {\n471:       const section = context.matched.find((entry) => entry.entry.id === pin.section);\n472:       if (!section) return max;\n473:       const end = deliveryAnchorEnd(section.section, pin);\n474:       return end === null ? max : Math.max(max, end);\n475:     }, 0);\n476: \n477:   const failures = [...own];\n478:   // The canary already names whatever it measured, so this row adds a figure only for a\n479:   // defect the canary did not report, instead of printing the same ceiling twice.\n480:   if (prefixEnd > limit && !own.some((line) => line.includes('delivery prefix'))) {\n481:     failures.push(`the last anchor ends at byte ${prefixEnd}, past the ${limit}-byte delivery prefix`);\n482:   }\n483:   if (context.totalBytes > ceiling && !own.some((line) => line.includes('ceiling'))) {\n484:     failures.push(`${context.totalBytes} bytes exceeds the ${ceiling}-byte ceiling`);\n485:   }\n486:   // A failing canary that names other files only is still a fact worth stating, because\n487:   // its exit status would otherwise read as this row's verdict. A passing one needs no\n488:   // note: its remaining lines are its own anchor table.\n489:   const notes = failures.length === 0 && run.status !== 0 && elsewhere.length > 0\n490:     ? [`the canary exits ${run.status} over ${elsewhere.length} line(s) outside ${manifest.root_file}, which belong to other files' pins`]\n491:     : [];\n492: \n493:   return row('A6', 'delivery budget', failures.length === 0 ? PASS : FINDING,\n494:     `${prefixEnd} of ${limit} bytes to the last anchor, ${context.totalBytes} of ${ceiling} bytes total`,\n495:     { lines: [...failures, ...notes] });\n496: }\n497: \n498: // A7. The repository block: one start marker line, one end marker line, in that order,\n499: // after every shipped section, small enough to leave headroom. A marker is the whole line,\n500: // so the same bytes quoted inside a sentence are prose and do not make a second block.\n501: function rowBlock(context, manifest) {\n502:   const { budget } = manifest;\n503:   const block = context.block;\n504:   const starts = block === null ? 0 : block.markerLines.start.length;\n505:   const ends = block === null ? 0 : block.markerLines.end.length;\n506: \n507:   if (starts === 0 && ends === 0) {\n508:     return row('A7', 'repo-local block', PASS, `no block; ${context.totalBytes} of ${budget.max_bytes} bytes used`);\n509:   }\n510:   if (starts !== 1 || ends !== 1) {\n511:     const lines = ['the block cannot be placed, so nothing inside it can be carried'];\n512:     const where = [];\n513:     if (block !== null && starts > 0) where.push(`the start marker sits on line ${block.markerLines.start.join(' and line ')}`);\n514:     if (block !== null && ends > 0) where.push(`the end marker on line ${block.markerLines.end.join(' and line ')}`);\n515:     if (where.length > 0) lines.push(where.join(', '));\n516:     return row('A7', 'repo-local block', FINDING,\n517:       `expected one start and one end marker line, found ${starts} and ${ends}`, { lines });\n518:   }\n519: \n520:   const lines = [];\n521:   if (!block || !block.complete) {\n522:     lines.push('the end marker sits before the start marker, so the block is out of order and unreadable');\n523:   }\n524:   // Whatever the document carries below the block is content a replacement has to place\n525:   // around the block, so a block above it cannot be the one block a rewrite writes last.\n526:   const following = block === null ? [] : context.sections.filter((section) => section.start > block.start);\n527:   if (block && block.complete && following.length > 0) {\n528:     lines.push(`the block starts at byte ${block.start}, before ${following.length} section(s) that follow it (the first is ${JSON.stringify(following[0].heading)} at byte ${following[0].start})`);\n529:   }\n530:   if (block && block.complete && block.start < budget.prefix_bytes) {\n531:     lines.push(`the block starts at byte ${block.start}, inside the ${budget.prefix_bytes}-byte delivery prefix`);\n532:   }\n533:   if (context.totalBytes > budget.max_bytes) {\n534:     lines.push(`${context.totalBytes} bytes exceeds the ${budget.max_bytes}-byte ceiling`);\n535:   }\n536: \n537:   const size = block && block.complete ? Buffer.byteLength(block.content, 'utf8') : 0;\n538:   const headroom = budget.max_bytes - context.totalBytes;\n539:   const detail = block && block.complete\n540:     ? `block ${size} bytes, ${headroom} bytes of headroom`\n541:     : `block unreadable, ${headroom} bytes of headroom`;\n542:   return row('A7', 'repo-local block', lines.length === 0 ? PASS : FINDING, detail,\n543:     { lines, entries: [{ size, headroom, complete: Boolean(block && block.complete) }] });\n544: }\n545: \n546: // A8. Repository content outside the block that matches no shipped section. This is the\n547: // content a replacement would drop, so it is reported by line and byte range. The text\n548: // above the first heading has no heading to match, and arrives here with a null one.\n549: function rowUnmarked(context, manifest) {\n550:   const prefixBytes = manifest.budget.prefix_bytes;\n551:   const entries = context.unregistered.map((entry) => ({\n552:     heading: entry.heading,\n553:     line_start: lineNumberAt(context.text, entry.start),\n554:     line_end: lineNumberAt(context.text, Math.max(entry.start, entry.end - 1)),\n555:     byte_start: entry.start,\n556:     byte_end: entry.end,\n557:     in_prefix: entry.start < prefixBytes,\n558:   }));\n559:   if (entries.length === 0) return row('A8', 'unmarked repo-unique content', PASS, 'no section outside the block');\n560: \n561:   const headings = entries.filter((entry) => entry.heading !== null).length;\n562:   const detail = [];\n563:   if (headings > 0) detail.push(`${headings} section(s) outside the block match no shipped section`);\n564:   if (entries.length > headings) detail.push('the text above the first heading is repository content no section carries');\n565: \n566:   return row('A8', 'unmarked repo-unique content', FINDING,\n567:     detail.join('; '),\n568:     {\n569:       lines: entries.map((entry) => `${entry.heading === null ? 'text before the first heading' : JSON.stringify(entry.heading)}: lines ${entry.line_start}-${entry.line_end}, bytes ${entry.byte_start}-${entry.byte_end}${entry.in_prefix ? ', inside the delivery prefix' : ''}`),\n570:       entries,\n571:     });\n572: }\n573: \n574: // The line where two bodies first part company, with the two raw lines. A body can carry\n575: // every line the shipped body carries and still not be it — an extra space on an indent, a\n576: // line written twice, two lines swapped — and the operator needs the line to look at\n577: // rather than a finding with nothing under it.\n578: function bodyDifference(actual, expected) {\n579:   const left = String(actual).split('\\n');\n580:   const right = String(expected).split('\\n');\n581:   const last = Math.max(left.length, right.length);\n582:   for (let index = 0; index < last; index += 1) {\n583:     if (left[index] === right[index]) continue;\n584:     return {\n585:       line: index + 1,\n586:       actual: left[index] === undefined ? null : left[index],\n587:       shipped: right[index] === undefined ? null : right[index],\n588:     };\n589:   }\n590:   return null;\n591: }\n592: \n593: // A9. For a section that is not the shipped body, the lines the shipped body does not\n594: // carry, once per occurrence. A body that carries every shipped line can still differ, so\n595: // the row names that difference by line instead of reporting a finding with no line under\n596: // it: a finding nobody can act on reads like a bug in the checker.\n597: function rowUnmarkedEdits(context, manifest) {\n598:   const entries = [];\n599:   for (const entry of context.currency) {\n600:     if (entry.state !== 'edited' && entry.state !== 'unknown-base') continue;\n601:     const shipped = manifest.derived.sections[entry.id];\n602:     const body = shipped ? shipped.body : '';\n603:     const lines = localLinesOf(entry.section.body, body);\n604:     entries.push({\n605:       id: entry.id,\n606:       state: entry.state,\n607:       lines,\n608:       difference: lines.length === 0 ? bodyDifference(entry.section.body, body) : null,\n609:     });\n610:   }\n611:   if (entries.length === 0) return row('A9', 'unmarked local edits', PASS, 'no edited section');\n612: \n613:   const total = entries.reduce((sum, entry) => sum + entry.lines.length, 0);\n614:   const formatting = entries.filter((entry) => entry.lines.length === 0).length;\n615:   const detail = [`${entries.length} section(s) carry ${total} local line(s)`];\n616:   if (formatting > 0) detail.push(`${formatting} of them differ only in whitespace, order or a repeat`);\n617: \n618:   const lines = [];\n619:   for (const entry of entries) {\n620:     for (const line of entry.lines) lines.push(`${entry.id}: ${line}`);\n621:     if (entry.difference === null) continue;\n622:     lines.push(`${entry.id}: only whitespace, order or a repeat differs from the shipped body, at line ${entry.difference.line}: shipped ${JSON.stringify(entry.difference.shipped)}, the document carries ${JSON.stringify(entry.difference.actual)}`);\n623:   }\n624:   return row('A9', 'unmarked local edits', lines.length === 0 ? INFO : FINDING,\n625:     detail.join('; '), { lines, entries });\n626: }\n627: \n628: // A10. The copies each runtime loads. Claude Code reaches the root document only through\n629: // CLAUDE.md, so a missing pointer leaves Claude without it and a full copy double-loads\n630: // and drifts. The Cursor rule is generated and alone carries the gate 1 lookup to Cursor\n631: // and Devin, so it is checked by its own generator.\n632: function rowRuntimeCopies(context, manifest, tools) {\n633:   const entries = [];\n634:   const lines = [];\n635: \n636:   for (const expected of manifest.runtime_copies || []) {\n637:     const verdict = copyVerdict(expected, context, manifest, tools);\n638:     entries.push({ path: expected.path, kind: verdict.kind, state: verdict.state, lines: verdict.lines || [] });\n639:     // A copy nobody could measure is printed like a finding: a pass line with no detail\n640:     // reads as a copy that was checked and found right.\n641:     if (verdict.state === FINDING || verdict.state === UNKNOWN || verdict.kind === 'pointer missing') {\n642:       lines.push(`${expected.path}: ${verdict.kind} - ${verdict.detail}`);\n643:     }\n644:   }\n645: \n646:   const failed = entries.filter((entry) => entry.state === FINDING);\n647:   const unmeasured = entries.filter((entry) => entry.state === UNKNOWN);\n648:   const state = failed.length > 0 ? FINDING : (unmeasured.length > 0 ? UNKNOWN : PASS);\n649:   const detail = `${entries.length - failed.length - unmeasured.length}/${entries.length} copies as expected${unmeasured.length > 0 ? `, ${unmeasured.length} unmeasured` : ''}`;\n650:   return row('A10', 'runtime copies', state, detail, { lines, entries });\n651: }\n652: \n653: function copyVerdict(expected, context, manifest, tools) {\n654:   if (expected.report_only) {\n655:     return { state: INFO, kind: 'reported only', detail: 'outside the checklist this command rewrites' };\n656:   }\n657:   if (expected.path.startsWith('~')) {\n658:     return { state: INFO, kind: 'outside the repository', detail: 'reported, never read' };\n659:   }\n660: \n661:   const target = path.join(context.root, expected.path);\n662:   if (expected.path.endsWith('.md') && expected.expect.includes('gate1-block-in-sync')) {\n663:     if (typeof tools.pointerSync !== 'string' || !fs.existsSync(tools.pointerSync)) {\n664:       return { state: UNKNOWN, kind: 'gate1 block unmeasured', detail: `the pointer sync is not installed at ${tools.pointerSync || '(no path)'}` };\n665:     }\n666:     const run = spawnSync(process.execPath, [tools.pointerSync, '--check', '--root', context.root], {\n667:       encoding: 'utf8',\n668:       timeout: TOOL_TIMEOUT_MS,\n669:     });\n670:     const output = `${run.stdout || ''}\\n${run.stderr || ''}`.split('\\n').map((line) => line.trim()).filter((line) => line !== '');\n671:     if (run.status === 0) return { state: PASS, kind: 'gate1 block in sync', detail: 'the generator reports no drift' };\n672:     if (run.status === 1) return { state: FINDING, kind: 'gate1 block out of sync', detail: output.join('; ') };\n673:     return { state: UNKNOWN, kind: 'gate1 block unmeasured', detail: output.join('; ') || `the pointer sync exited ${run.status}` };\n674:   }\n675: \n676:   const exists = fs.existsSync(target) || isSymlink(target);\n677:   const expectsAbsent = expected.expect.length === 1 && expected.expect[0] === 'absent';\n678: \n679:   if (expected.path === '.cursor/rules/repo-local.md') {\n680:     if (context.blockContent) {\n681:       if (!exists) return { state: FINDING, kind: 'mirror missing', detail: 'the block carries content and nothing mirrors it' };\n682:       const mirror = fs.readFileSync(target, 'utf8');\n683:       const missing = [...trimmedLines(context.block.content)].filter((line) => !mirror.includes(line));\n684:       return missing.length === 0\n685:         ? { state: PASS, kind: 'mirror current', detail: 'the mirror carries the block' }\n686:         : { state: FINDING, kind: 'mirror behind', detail: `${missing.length} block line(s) are not in the mirror` };\n687:     }\n688:     return exists\n689:       ? { state: FINDING, kind: 'mirror of an empty block', detail: 'the block is empty and a mirror exists' }\n690:       : { state: PASS, kind: 'absent', detail: 'no block content to mirror' };\n691:   }\n692: \n693:   // Claude Code reaches the root document only through this file, so its absence is a route\n694:   // the runtime does not have rather than a copy that is wrong.\n695:   if (expected.path === 'CLAUDE.md' && !exists && context.exists) {\n696:     return {\n697:       state: INFO,\n698:       kind: 'pointer missing',\n699:       detail: 'Claude Code reads only CLAUDE.md, so without a one-line @' + manifest.root_file + ' pointer it never loads ' + manifest.root_file,\n700:     };\n701:   }\n702: \n703:   if (!exists) {\n704:     return expectsAbsent\n705:       ? { state: PASS, kind: 'absent', detail: 'nothing to load twice' }\n706:       : { state: PASS, kind: 'absent', detail: 'absent, which the checklist allows' };\n707:   }\n708: \n709:   if (isSymlink(target)) {\n710:     const link = fs.readlinkSync(target);\n711:     // A path the checklist expects to be absent is one no runtime should load, and a link\n712:     // there loads the root document under that name just as a copy would.\n713:     if (expectsAbsent) {\n714:       return { state: FINDING, kind: 'symlink where none belongs', detail: `links to ${link}, where the checklist expects no file` };\n715:     }\n716:     const pointsAtRoot = path.basename(link) === manifest.root_file;\n717:     if (pointsAtRoot) return { state: PASS, kind: 'symlink', detail: `links to ${link}` };\n718:     return { state: FINDING, kind: 'symlink elsewhere', detail: `links to ${link} instead of ${manifest.root_file}` };\n719:   }\n720: \n721:   const content = fs.readFileSync(target, 'utf8');\n722:   if (!context.exists) {\n723:     return { state: FINDING, kind: 'sole instruction file', detail: `there is no ${manifest.root_file} for it to point at` };\n724:   }\n725:   if (content === context.text) {\n726:     return { state: FINDING, kind: 'identical copy', detail: `byte-identical to ${manifest.root_file}, so the two will drift` };\n727:   }\n728: \n729:   const unique = localLinesOf(content, context.text);\n730:   const rootLines = trimmedLines(context.text);\n731:   const sharesShippedLine = [...trimmedLines(content)].some((line) => rootLines.has(line));\n732:   const namesRoot = [...trimmedLines(content)].some((line) => line.includes(manifest.root_file));\n733:   // A pointer is a file that carries no shipped line and names the root document. Anything\n734:   // else is either a copy or a copy plus local lines, and both drift: the second also\n735:   // loses text when it is replaced, so the lines only it carries are listed.\n736:   if (!sharesShippedLine && namesRoot) {\n737:     return { state: PASS, kind: 'pointer', detail: `every line names ${manifest.root_file}` };\n738:   }\n739:   return { state: FINDING, kind: 'divergent copy', detail: `${unique.length} line(s) only this file carries`, lines: unique };\n740: }\n741: \n742: function isSymlink(target) {\n743:   try {\n744:     return fs.lstatSync(target).isSymbolicLink();\n745:   } catch {\n746:     return false;\n747:   }\n748: }\n749: \n750: // A11. The router: present, carrying the rows the template ships, and passing its own\n751: // checker. The checker's summary line is carried through, never rebuilt.\n752: function rowRouter(context, manifest, tools) {\n753:   const routerPath = path.join(context.root, manifest.router_file);\n754:   if (!fs.existsSync(routerPath)) {\n755:     return row('A11', 'REPO RULES.md', FINDING, 'absent',\n756:       { lines: [`no ${manifest.router_file}: no repository rule can load`], entries: [{ path: manifest.router_file, present: false }] });\n757:   }\n758: \n759:   const text = fs.readFileSync(routerPath, 'utf8');\n760:   const shipped = (manifest.derived && manifest.derived.repo_rules) || { trigger_rows: [], index_rows: [] };\n761:   const lines = [];\n762:   const missing = [\n763:     ...missingRows(shipped.trigger_rows, parseTableRows(text, 'TRIGGER TABLE'), 'trigger'),\n764:     ...missingRows(shipped.index_rows, parseTableRows(text, 'INDEX'), 'index'),\n765:   ];\n766:   for (const entry of missing) lines.push(`missing ${entry.kind} row for ${entry.rule}`);\n767: \n768:   if (typeof tools.repoRules !== 'string' || !fs.existsSync(tools.repoRules)) {\n769:     return row('A11', 'REPO RULES.md', UNKNOWN, 'present; the repo-rules checker is not installed',\n770:       { lines: [...lines, `the checker is not installed at ${tools.repoRules || '(no path)'}`] });\n771:   }\n772: \n773:   const run = spawnSync(process.execPath, [tools.repoRules, '--root', context.root], {\n774:     encoding: 'utf8',\n775:     timeout: TOOL_TIMEOUT_MS,\n776:   });\n777:   const output = `${run.stdout || ''}\\n${run.stderr || ''}`.split('\\n').map((line) => line.trim()).filter((line) => line !== '');\n778:   const summary = output.find((line) => line.includes(REPO_RULES_TAG) && line.includes('RESULT:'));\n779: \n780:   if (run.status === 2 || !summary) {\n781:     return row('A11', 'REPO RULES.md', UNKNOWN, 'the repo-rules checker could not run',\n782:       { lines: [...lines, ...output.filter((line) => line.includes(REPO_RULES_TAG))] });\n783:   }\n784:   if (run.status !== 0) {\n785:     return row('A11', 'REPO RULES.md', FINDING, `${missing.length} shipped row(s) missing; the checker fails`,\n786:       { lines: [...lines, summary] });\n787:   }\n788:   return row('A11', 'REPO RULES.md', lines.length === 0 ? PASS : FINDING,\n789:     `${missing.length} shipped row(s) missing; ${summary}`, { lines: [...lines, summary] });\n790: }\n791: \n792: // The router's own table, read the same way the manifest's rows were derived. Comparing\n793: // shipped rows to the file is the only way to say which of them a consumer dropped.\n794: function parseTableRows(text, anchor) {\n795:   const lines = text.split('\\n');\n796:   const start = lines.findIndex((line) => line.startsWith('#') && line.includes(anchor));\n797:   if (start === -1) return [];\n798: \n799:   const rows = [];\n800:   let width = 0;\n801:   for (let index = start + 1; index < lines.length; index += 1) {\n802:     const line = lines[index].trim();\n803:     if (line.startsWith('#')) break;\n804:     if (!line.startsWith('|')) {\n805:       if (rows.length > 0 && line === '') break;\n806:       continue;\n807:     }\n808:     const cells = line.replace(/^\\|/u, '').replace(/\\|$/u, '').split('|').map((cell) => cell.trim());\n809:     if (cells.every((cell) => /^:?-+:?$/u.test(cell) || cell === '')) continue;\n810:     if (width === 0) {\n811:       width = cells.length;\n812:       continue;\n813:     }\n814:     if (cells.length !== width) continue;\n815:     rows.push(cells);\n816:   }\n817:   return rows;\n818: }\n819: \n820: function rulePathOf(cells) {\n821:   for (const cell of cells) {\n822:     const linked = /\\(([^)]+\\.md)\\)/u.exec(cell);\n823:     if (linked) return linked[1];\n824:     const bare = /`([^`]+\\.md)`/u.exec(cell);\n825:     if (bare) return bare[1];\n826:   }\n827:   return null;\n828: }\n829: \n830: function missingRows(shipped, present, kind) {\n831:   const found = new Set(present.map((cells) => rulePathOf(cells) || cells.join(' | ')));\n832:   const missing = [];\n833:   for (const cells of shipped) {\n834:     const rule = rulePathOf(cells) || cells.join(' | ');\n835:     if (!found.has(rule)) missing.push({ kind, rule });\n836:   }\n837:   return missing;\n838: }\n839: \n840: // A12. The source repository's own two promises: its block stays empty, and its derived\n841: // copy of the template is fresh. A consumer's block is its own, so this row reports INFO\n842: // there instead of telling it what to do with its own file.\n843: function rowTemplatePurity(context, manifest, tools, options) {\n844:   let source = { isSource: false, reason: `origin was not compared` };\n845:   try {\n846:     source = sourceStatus({ root: context.root, manifest });\n847:   } catch (error) {\n848:     source = { isSource: false, reason: error.message };\n849:   }\n850: \n851:   if (!options.source && !source.isSource) {\n852:     return row('A12', 'template purity', INFO,\n853:       `runs only in the source repository; ${source.reason || 'the origin differs'}`);\n854:   }\n855: \n856:   const lines = [];\n857:   if (context.blockContent) lines.push('the source repository block carries content, and every line here ships to every consumer');\n858:   if (context.block && !context.block.complete) lines.push('the source repository block is not well formed');\n859: \n860:   if (typeof tools.generator !== 'string' || !fs.existsSync(tools.generator)) {\n861:     return row('A12', 'template purity', UNKNOWN,\n862:       `the manifest generator is not installed at ${tools.generator || '(no path)'}`);\n863:   }\n864:   const run = spawnSync(process.execPath, [tools.generator, '--check', '--root', context.root, '--manifest', options.manifest], {\n865:     encoding: 'utf8',\n866:     timeout: TOOL_TIMEOUT_MS,\n867:   });\n868:   const output = `${run.stdout || ''}\\n${run.stderr || ''}`.split('\\n').map((line) => line.trim()).filter((line) => line !== '');\n869:   if (run.status !== 0) lines.push(...output);\n870: \n871:   const state = lines.length === 0 ? PASS : FINDING;\n872:   return row('A12', 'template purity', state,\n873:     state === PASS ? 'block empty and the derived copy is fresh' : 'the source template is not pure', { lines });\n874: }\n875: \n876: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n877: // 6. REPORT\n878: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n879: \n880: function defaultTools(manifest) {\n881:   const canaryPin = (manifest.pins || []).find((pin) => String(pin.consumer || '').endsWith('check-rule-copies.js'));\n882:   const pointerPin = (manifest.pins || []).find((pin) => pin.kind === 'first-occurrence');\n883:   return {\n884:     canary: canaryPin ? path.join(REPO_ROOT, canaryPin.consumer) : null,\n885:     pointerSync: pointerPin ? path.join(REPO_ROOT, pointerPin.consumer) : null,\n886:     repoRules: REPO_RULES_CHECKER,\n887:     generator: GENERATOR,\n888:   };\n889: }\n890: \n891: /**\n892:  * Classify one repository.\n893:  *\n894:  * @param {{root?: string, manifestPath?: string, source?: boolean, tools?: Object}} [input]\n895:  * @returns {{root: string, manifest: string, state: string, status: string, exitCode: number,\n896:  *   findings: number, unknown: number, rows: Array<Object>} | null} Null when the root\n897:  *   directory or the manifest is not readable.\n898:  */\n899: function buildReport(input = {}) {\n900:   const root = input.root ? path.resolve(input.root) : REPO_ROOT;\n901:   const manifestPath = input.manifestPath ? path.resolve(input.manifestPath) : MANIFEST_PATH;\n902:   if (!fs.existsSync(manifestPath)) return null;\n903: \n904:   let manifest;\n905:   try {\n906:     manifest = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(manifestPath, 'utf8'));\n907:   } catch {\n908:     return null;\n909:   }\n910: \n911:   const context = readTarget({ root, manifest });\n912:   if (context === null) return null;\n913: \n914:   const tools = { ...defaultTools(manifest), ...(input.tools || {}) };\n915:   const rows = [\n916:     rowInstallState(context, manifest),\n917:     rowSectionsPresent(context, manifest),\n918:     rowOrderAndNumbering(context, manifest),\n919:     rowCurrency(context),\n920:     rowPins(context, manifest),\n921:     rowBudget(context, manifest, tools),\n922:     rowBlock(context, manifest),\n923:     rowUnmarked(context, manifest),\n924:     rowUnmarkedEdits(context, manifest),\n925:     rowRuntimeCopies(context, manifest, tools),\n926:     rowRouter(context, manifest, tools),\n927:     rowTemplatePurity(context, manifest, tools, { source: Boolean(input.source), manifest: manifestPath }),\n928:   ];\n929: \n930:   const findings = rows.filter((entry) => entry.state === FINDING).length;\n931:   const unknown = rows.filter((entry) => entry.state === UNKNOWN).length;\n932:   const exitCode = findings > 0 || unknown > 0 ? 1 : 0;\n933:   const status = exitCode === 0 ? 'OK' : (findings > 0 ? 'FINDINGS' : 'UNKNOWN');\n934: \n935:   return {\n936:     root,\n937:     manifest: manifestPath,\n938:     state: rows[0].detail,\n939:     status,\n940:     exitCode,\n941:     findings,\n942:     unknown,\n943:     rows,\n944:   };\n945: }\n946: \n947: // A long row is a list, not a wall: the report keeps every line in the JSON form and\n948: // prints the first few here, so a reader sees the shape before the volume.\n949: const PRINTED_LINES = 20;\n950: \n951: function renderText(report) {\n952:   const lines = [];\n953:   for (const entry of report.rows) {\n954:     lines.push(`${entry.id} ${entry.name}: ${entry.detail}   (${entry.state})`);\n955:     for (const line of entry.lines.slice(0, PRINTED_LINES)) lines.push(`  ${line}`);\n956:     if (entry.lines.length > PRINTED_LINES) {\n957:       lines.push(`  ... ${entry.lines.length - PRINTED_LINES} more line(s), all of them in the JSON report`);\n958:     }\n959:   }\n960:   lines.push(`STATUS=${report.status} rows=${report.rows.length} findings=${report.findings} unknown=${report.unknown}`);\n961:   return `${lines.join('\\n')}\\n`;\n962: }\n963: \n964: function renderJson(report) {\n965:   const shaped = {\n966:     root: report.root,\n967:     manifest: report.manifest,\n968:     state: report.state,\n969:     status: report.status,\n970:     exit_code: report.exitCode,\n971:     findings: report.findings,\n972:     unknown: report.unknown,\n973:     rows: report.rows.map((entry) => ({\n974:       id: entry.id,\n975:       name: entry.name,\n976:       state: entry.state,\n977:       detail: entry.detail,\n978:       lines: entry.lines,\n979:       entries: entry.entries,\n980:     })),\n981:   };\n982:   return `${JSON.stringify(shaped, null, 2)}\\n`;\n983: }\n984: \n985: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n986: // 7. MAIN\n987: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n988: \n989: /**\n990:  * @param {string[]} argv\n991:  * @param {{tools?: Object}} [hooks] - test seams for the child tools\n992:  * @returns {number} the process exit code\n993:  */\n994: function main(argv, hooks = {}) {\n995:   const options = parseArgs(argv);\n996:   if (options.help) {\n997:     console.log(USAGE);\n998:     return 0;\n999:   }\n1000: \n1001:   if (!fs.existsSync(options.manifest)) {\n1002:     console.error(`${TAG} ERROR: the manifest is missing at ${options.manifest}`);\n1003:     return 2;\n1004:   }\n1005:   if (!fs.existsSync(options.root) || !fs.statSync(options.root).isDirectory()) {\n1006:     console.error(`${TAG} ERROR: the target root is not a directory: ${options.root}`);\n1007:     return 2;\n1008:   }\n1009:   // The root file's name is the manifest's to declare, so this precondition reads the same\n1010:   // declaration every other read does instead of a name of its own.\n1011:   let rootFile = null;\n1012:   try {\n1013:     rootFile = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(options.manifest, 'utf8')).root_file || null;\n1014:   } catch {\n1015:     // An unreadable manifest is refused below, where the run has no report to print.\n1016:     rootFile = null;\n1017:   }\n1018:   const rootPath = rootFile === null ? null : path.join(options.root, rootFile);\n1019:   if (rootPath !== null && fs.existsSync(rootPath)) {\n1020:     try {\n1021:       fs.readFileSync(rootPath, 'utf8');\n1022:     } catch (error) {\n1023:       console.error(`${TAG} ERROR: cannot read ${rootPath}: ${error.message}`);\n1024:       return 2;\n1025:     }\n1026:   }\n1027: \n1028:   const report = buildReport({\n1029:     root: options.root,\n1030:     manifestPath: options.manifest,\n1031:     source: options.source,\n1032:     tools: hooks.tools || {},\n1033:   });\n1034:   if (report === null) {\n1035:     // A manifest that cannot be parsed leaves nothing to compare against, and an empty\n1036:     // report would read like a clean one.\n1037:     console.error(`${TAG} ERROR: cannot classify ${options.root}: the manifest at ${options.manifest} could not be read as JSON`);\n1038:     return 2;\n1039:   }\n1040: \n1041:   process.stdout.write(options.json ? renderJson(report) : renderText(report));\n1042:   return report.exitCode;\n1043: }\n1044: \n1045: if (require.main === module) {\n1046:   try {\n1047:     process.exitCode = main(process.argv.slice(2));\n1048:   } catch (error) {\n1049:     console.error(`${TAG} ERROR: ${error.message}`);\n1050:     if (error instanceof UsageError) console.error(USAGE);\n1051:     process.exitCode = 2;\n1052:   }\n1053: }\n1054: \n1055: module.exports = {\n1056:   MANIFEST_PATH,\n1057:   buildReport,\n1058:   main,\n1059:   renderJson,\n1060:   renderText,\n1061: };","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-check.cjs, lines 1-1061\n1: #!/usr/bin/env node\n2: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n3: // MODULE: Shipped Instruction Check Engine\n4: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n5: 'use strict';\n6: \n7: // A repository that took the shipped instruction template can be in any of seven\n8: // states, and nothing in it says which one. Its shipped sections may be current, one\n9: // release behind, or edited in place; its own content may sit in the marked block or be\n10: // scattered between shipped sections; it may have no root document at all. Without that\n11: // answer there is nothing to plan a rewrite from, and no way to tell a safe replacement\n12: // from one that would drop a line somebody wrote. So this engine reports, per row and\n13: // without writing a byte, what the repository holds against the manifest.\n14: //\n15: // Three of the twelve rows are answers this engine must not invent. The delivery budget\n16: // and its anchors belong to the rule-copy canary, the Cursor pointer block belongs to\n17: // the gate 1 sync, and the router belongs to the repo-rules checker. Each already has\n18: // tests and an owner, so each is run as a child process and its own lines are carried\n19: // into the report; re-deriving any of them here would be a second opinion that drifts\n20: // from the first. The rest are pure classifiers over one read of the target tree.\n21: //\n22: // The run is read-only by construction: the only paths it opens for reading are under\n23: // the target root plus the manifest, and the three child tools it runs are read-only\n24: // modes. A repository that cannot be classified at all (no manifest, no target\n25: // directory, an unreadable root document) is a failed precondition and exits 2, because\n26: // an empty report would read like a clean one.\n27: //\n28: // Usage: agents-md-check.cjs [--root <dir>] [--manifest <path>] [--source] [--json]\n29: //   --root <dir>      classify this repository instead of the one this script ships in\n30: //   --manifest <path> read this manifest instead of the shipped one\n31: //   --source          run the template-purity row even outside the source repository\n32: //   --json            print one JSON object instead of the text report\n33: // Exit codes: 0 nothing to report, 1 at least one finding or an unproven row,\n34: //             2 a precondition failed\n35: \n36: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n37: // 1. IMPORTS\n38: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n39: \n40: const { spawnSync } = require('node:child_process');\n41: const fs = require('node:fs');\n42: const path = require('node:path');\n43: \n44: const {\n45:   classifyBody,\n46:   deliveryAnchorEnd,\n47:   matchSections,\n48:   segment,\n49: } = require('./lib/agents-md-sections.cjs');\n50: const { sourceStatus } = require('./agents-md-manifest.cjs');\n51: \n52: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n53: // 2. CONSTANTS\n54: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n55: \n56: const TAG = '[agents-md-check]';\n57: const REPO_ROOT = path.resolve(__dirname, '../../../..');\n58: const MANIFEST_PATH = path.resolve(__dirname, '../assets/agents-md-manifest.json');\n59: const GENERATOR = path.resolve(__dirname, 'agents-md-manifest.cjs');\n60: // The router checker ships beside the skill that authors rules, and the manifest links\n61: // it only through the router's own row, so its path is named here once.\n62: const REPO_RULES_CHECKER = path.resolve(\n63:   REPO_ROOT,\n64:   '.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-repo-rule/scripts/check-repo-rules.cjs',\n65: );\n66: // The checker's own tag prefix. Matching on it is what keeps this row a carrier of that\n67: // tool's verdict instead of a second implementation of its checks.\n68: const REPO_RULES_TAG = '[repo-rules-check]';\n69: const USAGE = 'Usage: node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-check.cjs [--root <dir>] [--manifest <path>] [--source] [--json]';\n70: const TOOL_TIMEOUT_MS = 120000;\n71: \n72: // A repository where fewer than half the shipped sections survive is carrying its own\n73: // document, not a copy of the template. Above the line the question becomes which parts\n74: // of the template are still there, which is a per-section answer.\n75: const FOREIGN_SHARE = 0.5;\n76: \n77: const PASS = 'PASS';\n78: const FINDING = 'FINDING';\n79: const UNKNOWN = 'UNKNOWN';\n80: const INFO = 'INFO';\n81: \n82: class UsageError extends Error {}\n83: \n84: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n85: // 3. ARGUMENTS\n86: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n87: \n88: function parseArgs(argv) {\n89:   const options = { root: REPO_ROOT, manifest: MANIFEST_PATH, source: false, json: false, help: false };\n90:   for (let index = 0; index < argv.length; index += 1) {\n91:     const argument = argv[index];\n92:     if (argument === '--root') {\n93:       const value = argv[index + 1];\n94:       if (!value || value.startsWith('--')) throw new UsageError('--root needs a directory');\n95:       options.root = path.resolve(value);\n96:       index += 1;\n97:     } else if (argument === '--manifest') {\n98:       const value = argv[index + 1];\n99:       if (!value || value.startsWith('--')) throw new UsageError('--manifest needs a file path');\n100:       options.manifest = path.resolve(value);\n101:       index += 1;\n102:     } else if (argument === '--source') {\n103:       options.source = true;\n104:     } else if (argument === '--json') {\n105:       options.json = true;\n106:     } else if (argument === '--help' || argument === '-h') {\n107:       options.help = true;\n108:     } else {\n109:       throw new UsageError(`unknown argument: ${argument}`);\n110:     }\n111:   }\n112:   return options;\n113: }\n114: \n115: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n116: // 4. READING THE TARGET ONCE\n117: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n118: \n119: // Section offsets are byte offsets because the delivery budget is a byte count, while a\n120: // reader opens the file by line number. One conversion, in one place.\n121: function lineNumberAt(text, byteOffset) {\n122:   return Buffer.from(text, 'utf8').subarray(0, byteOffset).toString('utf8').split('\\n').length;\n123: }\n124: \n125: function trimmedLines(text) {\n126:   const lines = new Set();\n127:   for (const raw of String(text).split('\\n')) {\n128:     const line = raw.trim();\n129:     if (line !== '' && line !== '---') lines.add(line);\n130:   }\n131:   return lines;\n132: }\n133: \n134: // The repository's own lines, in order and with repeats. A set would report one line for\n135: // two occurrences, and a replacement loses both, so a duplicate is a second line to keep.\n136: function localLinesOf(body, reference) {\n137:   const known = trimmedLines(reference);\n138:   const local = [];\n139:   for (const raw of body.split('\\n')) {\n140:     const line = raw.trim();\n141:     if (line === '' || line === '---' || known.has(line)) continue;\n142:     local.push(line);\n143:   }\n144:   return local;\n145: }\n146: \n147: function knownBodies(manifest) {\n148:   const sections = manifest.derived && manifest.derived.sections ? manifest.derived.sections : {};\n149:   return {\n150:     current: Object.keys(sections).map((id) => ({ id, sha256: sections[id].sha256, body: sections[id].body })),\n151:     history: (manifest.derived && manifest.derived.history) || [],\n152:   };\n153: }\n154: \n155: // The block carries a heading and a note that the template itself ships, so \"the block is\n156: // empty\" means nothing beyond those two lines, not that the markers bracket no text.\n157: function blockHasContent(block, markers) {\n158:   if (!block || !block.complete) return false;\n159:   const shipped = new Set([markers.heading.trim(), markers.note.trim()]);\n160:   for (const raw of block.content.split('\\n')) {\n161:     const line = raw.trim();\n162:     if (line === '' || shipped.has(line)) continue;\n163:     return true;\n164:   }\n165:   return false;\n166: }\n167: \n168: /**\n169:  * Read the target tree once and keep what every row needs.\n170:  *\n171:  * @param {{root: string, manifest: Object}} input\n172:  * @returns {Object|null} Null when the root directory is not there.\n173:  */\n174: function readTarget({ root, manifest }) {\n175:   if (!fs.existsSync(root) || !fs.statSync(root).isDirectory()) return null;\n176: \n177:   const rootPath = path.join(root, manifest.root_file);\n178:   const exists = fs.existsSync(rootPath);\n179:   const text = exists ? fs.readFileSync(rootPath, 'utf8') : null;\n180:   const segmentation = exists\n181:     ? segment(text, manifest.markers, manifest.sections)\n182:     : { sections: [], block: null, prelude: null };\n183:   const pairing = matchSections(segmentation.sections, manifest.sections, segmentation.prelude);\n184: \n185:   return {\n186:     root,\n187:     rootPath,\n188:     exists,\n189:     text,\n190:     totalBytes: exists ? Buffer.byteLength(text, 'utf8') : 0,\n191:     block: segmentation.block,\n192:     prelude: segmentation.prelude,\n193:     sections: segmentation.sections,\n194:     matched: pairing.matched,\n195:     unregistered: pairing.unregistered,\n196:     unmatched: pairing.unmatched,\n197:     conflicts: pairing.conflicts,\n198:     currency: pairing.matched.map((row) => ({\n199:       id: row.entry.id,\n200:       section: row.section,\n201:       ...classifyBody(row.section.body, knownBodies(manifest)),\n202:     })),\n203:     blockContent: blockHasContent(segmentation.block, manifest.markers),\n204:   };\n205: }\n206: \n207: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n208: // 5. THE TWELVE ROWS\n209: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n210: \n211: function row(id, name, state, detail, extra = {}) {\n212:   return { id, name, state, detail, lines: extra.lines || [], entries: extra.entries || [] };\n213: }\n214: \n215: // A1. The one row an operator reads first: which of the seven shapes this repository is.\n216: function rowInstallState(context, manifest) {\n217:   const claudePath = path.join(context.root, 'CLAUDE.md');\n218:   if (!context.exists) {\n219:     if (fs.existsSync(claudePath)) {\n220:       return row('A1', 'install state', FINDING, 'claude-only',\n221:         { lines: [`no ${manifest.root_file}; CLAUDE.md is the only instruction file`] });\n222:     }\n223:     return row('A1', 'install state', FINDING, 'absent',\n224:       { lines: [`no ${manifest.root_file} and no CLAUDE.md`] });\n225:   }\n226: \n227:   const share = manifest.sections.length === 0 ? 0 : context.matched.length / manifest.sections.length;\n228:   if (share < FOREIGN_SHARE) {\n229:     return row('A1', 'install state', FINDING, 'foreign', {\n230:       lines: [\n231:         `${context.matched.length} of ${manifest.sections.length} shipped sections match, under the ${Math.round(FOREIGN_SHARE * 100)}% floor`,\n232:         'the root document is the repository\\'s own file',\n233:       ],\n234:     });\n235:   }\n236: \n237:   const localEdits = context.currency.filter((entry) => entry.state === 'edited' || entry.state === 'unknown-base');\n238:   const behind = context.currency.filter((entry) => entry.state === 'stale-known');\n239:   // A local edit outranks a missed release in the reading order: it is the shape that\n240:   // loses text if somebody replaces the document, and staleness only loses a patch.\n241:   if (localEdits.length > 0 || context.unregistered.length > 0) {\n242:     const lines = [];\n243:     for (const entry of localEdits) lines.push(`${entry.id}: ${entry.state}`);\n244:     for (const entry of context.unregistered) {\n245:       lines.push(entry.heading === null\n246:         ? 'unmarked text before the first heading'\n247:         : `unmarked section ${JSON.stringify(entry.heading)}`);\n248:     }\n249:     return row('A1', 'install state', FINDING, 'forked', { lines });\n250:   }\n251:   if (behind.length > 0) {\n252:     return row('A1', 'install state', FINDING, 'stale',\n253:       { lines: behind.map((entry) => `${entry.id}: ${entry.state}`) });\n254:   }\n255:   if (context.blockContent) {\n256:     return row('A1', 'install state', PASS, 'adopted',\n257:       { lines: ['every shipped section is current and the repository block carries content'] });\n258:   }\n259:   return row('A1', 'install state', PASS, 'pure',\n260:     { lines: ['every shipped section is current and the repository block is empty'] });\n261: }\n262: \n263: // A2. Which shipped sections survived, by the prefix each authored entry carries.\n264: function rowSectionsPresent(context, manifest) {\n265:   const present = new Set(context.matched.map((row_) => row_.entry.id));\n266:   const missing = manifest.sections.map((entry) => entry.id).filter((id) => !present.has(id));\n267:   const detail = `${present.size}/${manifest.sections.length} matched`;\n268:   if (missing.length === 0 && context.conflicts.length === 0) {\n269:     return row('A2', 'sections present', PASS, detail);\n270:   }\n271:   const lines = missing.map((id) => `missing section ${id}`);\n272:   for (const conflict of context.conflicts) lines.push(conflict.message);\n273:   return row('A2', 'sections present', FINDING, `${present.size}/${manifest.sections.length} matched, ${missing.length} missing`, {\n274:     lines,\n275:     entries: missing.map((id) => ({ id, present: false })),\n276:   });\n277: }\n278: \n279: // The byte offset of the first line that is exactly this text, or -1 when the document\n280: // does not carry it.\n281: function firstLineOffset(text, needle) {\n282:   if (text.startsWith(needle)) return 0;\n283:   const at = text.indexOf(`\\n${needle}`);\n284:   return at === -1 ? -1 : at + 1;\n285: }\n286: \n287: // The shipped numbering, read from the pin that names it. Hundreds of files cite these\n288: // numbers, and the document carries its fourth section before its third, so the citation\n289: // order is not the reading order and only the pin carries the expected one.\n290: function numberingReport(context, manifest) {\n291:   if (!context.exists) return { state: INFO, detail: 'no root document to number', lines: [] };\n292: \n293:   const pin = (manifest.pins || []).find((entry) => entry.kind === 'order');\n294:   if (!pin) return { state: INFO, detail: 'the manifest declares no numbering pin', lines: [] };\n295: \n296:   const found = [];\n297:   const absent = [];\n298:   for (const text of pin.texts) {\n299:     const at = firstLineOffset(context.text, text);\n300:     if (at === -1) absent.push(text.trim());\n301:     else found.push({ text: text.trim(), at });\n302:   }\n303:   const expected = pin.texts.map((text) => text.trim()).filter((text) => found.some((entry) => entry.text === text));\n304:   const actual = found.slice().sort((left, right) => left.at - right.at).map((entry) => entry.text);\n305: \n306:   const lines = absent.map((text) => `missing section number ${JSON.stringify(text)}`);\n307:   if (expected.join('|') !== actual.join('|')) {\n308:     lines.push(`the numbers appear as ${actual.join(', ')} instead of ${expected.join(', ')}`);\n309:   }\n310:   return { state: lines.length === 0 ? PASS : FINDING, detail: `${found.length}/${pin.texts.length} section numbers in place`, lines };\n311: }\n312: \n313: // A3. Shipped sections in manifest order, and section numbers unchanged.\n314: function rowOrderAndNumbering(context, manifest) {\n315:   const order = manifest.sections.map((entry) => entry.id);\n316:   const seen = context.matched.map((entry) => entry.entry.id);\n317:   const expected = order.filter((id) => seen.includes(id));\n318:   const numbering = numberingReport(context, manifest);\n319: \n320:   const lines = [...numbering.lines];\n321:   if (expected.join('|') !== seen.join('|')) {\n322:     const first = seen.findIndex((id, index) => id !== expected[index]);\n323:     lines.unshift(`section order differs at position ${first + 1}: found ${seen[first]}, expected ${expected[first]}`);\n324:   }\n325:   const state = lines.length === 0 ? PASS : FINDING;\n326:   return row('A3', 'order and numbering', state, state === PASS ? 'manifest order and section numbers unchanged' : 'order or numbering drifted', { lines });\n327: }\n328: \n329: // A4. Per section, whether the body is the shipped one, an older release, a local edit\n330: // or something with no base to name.\n331: function rowCurrency(context) {\n332:   const counts = { current: 0, 'stale-known': 0, edited: 0, 'unknown-base': 0 };\n333:   const entries = [];\n334:   for (const entry of context.currency) {\n335:     counts[entry.state] += 1;\n336:     entries.push({ id: entry.id, state: entry.state, base: entry.base || null });\n337:   }\n338:   // Nothing to classify is not a clean bill: the rows around this one report the empty or\n339:   // foreign document, and a pass here would read as currency that was checked.\n340:   if (entries.length === 0) {\n341:     return row('A4', 'section currency', INFO, 'no shipped section matched, so there is no body to classify');\n342:   }\n343:   const detail = `${counts.current} current, ${counts['stale-known']} stale-known, ${counts.edited} edited, ${counts['unknown-base']} unknown-base`;\n344:   const lines = [];\n345:   for (const entry of context.currency) {\n346:     if (entry.state === 'current') continue;\n347:     const base = entry.base ? entry.base.slice(0, 12) : 'none';\n348:     lines.push(`${entry.id}: ${entry.state} (closest known body ${base})`);\n349:   }\n350:   return row('A4', 'section currency', lines.length === 0 ? PASS : FINDING, detail, { lines, entries });\n351: }\n352: \n353: // A5. Every string downstream tooling greps out of the document, checked by its rule.\n354: function rowPins(context, manifest) {\n355:   const entries = [];\n356:   const byId = new Map(context.matched.map((entry) => [entry.entry.id, entry.section]));\n357: \n358:   for (const pin of manifest.pins || []) {\n359:     const verdict = pinVerdict(pin, context, manifest, byId);\n360:     entries.push({ id: pin.id, kind: pin.kind, state: verdict.state, detail: verdict.detail });\n361:   }\n362: \n363:   const failed = entries.filter((entry) => entry.state === FINDING);\n364:   const unmeasured = entries.filter((entry) => entry.state === UNKNOWN);\n365:   const lines = failed.map((entry) => `${entry.id} (${entry.kind}): ${entry.detail}`);\n366:   for (const entry of unmeasured) lines.push(`${entry.id} (${entry.kind}): ${entry.detail}`);\n367:   const state = failed.length > 0 ? FINDING : (unmeasured.length > 0 ? UNKNOWN : PASS);\n368:   const detail = `${entries.length - failed.length - unmeasured.length}/${entries.length} pins hold${unmeasured.length > 0 ? `, ${unmeasured.length} unmeasured` : ''}`;\n369:   return row('A5', 'pins', state, detail, { lines, entries });\n370: }\n371: \n372: function pinVerdict(pin, context, manifest, byId) {\n373:   const section = pin.section ? byId.get(pin.section) : null;\n374:   if (pin.section && !section) {\n375:     return { state: FINDING, detail: `the section ${pin.section} that carries this pin is missing` };\n376:   }\n377: \n378:   switch (pin.kind) {\n379:     case 'exact': {\n380:       const missing = pin.texts.filter((text) => !section.body.includes(text));\n381:       return missing.length === 0\n382:         ? { state: PASS, detail: `all ${pin.texts.length} strings present in ${pin.section}` }\n383:         : { state: FINDING, detail: `missing ${JSON.stringify(missing[0])} in ${pin.section}` };\n384:     }\n385:     case 'line': {\n386:       const required = (pin.requires || pin.texts).map((text) => text.toLowerCase());\n387:       const hit = section.body.split('\\n').some((line) => {\n388:         const lower = line.toLowerCase();\n389:         return required.every((text) => lower.includes(text));\n390:       });\n391:       return hit\n392:         ? { state: PASS, detail: `a line in ${pin.section} carries ${required.join(', ')}` }\n393:         : { state: FINDING, detail: `no line in ${pin.section} carries ${required.join(', ')}` };\n394:     }\n395:     case 'first-occurrence': {\n396:       const at = context.text.indexOf(pin.texts[0]);\n397:       const inside = at >= section.start && at < section.end;\n398:       if (inside) return { state: PASS, detail: `the first occurrence sits inside ${pin.section}` };\n399:       return {\n400:         state: FINDING,\n401:         detail: at === -1\n402:           ? `the text ${JSON.stringify(pin.texts[0])} is absent`\n403:           : `the first occurrence at byte ${at} sits outside ${pin.section} (${section.start}..${section.end}), so anything reading the first line of the document follows it instead`,\n404:       };\n405:     }\n406:     case 'delivery-anchor': {\n407:       const end = deliveryAnchorEnd(section, pin);\n408:       if (end === null) return { state: FINDING, detail: `the anchor text is absent from ${pin.section}` };\n409:       const limit = manifest.budget.prefix_bytes;\n410:       return end <= limit\n411:         ? { state: PASS, detail: `${pin.section} ends at byte ${end}, inside ${limit}` }\n412:         : { state: FINDING, detail: `${pin.section} ends at byte ${end}, past the ${limit}-byte delivery prefix` };\n413:     }\n414:     case 'budget': {\n415:       const declared = manifest.budget[pin.limit_key];\n416:       const pinned = Number(pin.texts[0]);\n417:       return declared === pinned\n418:         ? { state: PASS, detail: `${pin.limit_key} is ${declared}` }\n419:         : { state: FINDING, detail: `${pin.limit_key} reads ${declared}, the pin says ${pinned}` };\n420:     }\n421:     case 'order': {\n422:       const numbering = numberingReport(context, manifest);\n423:       return { state: numbering.state, detail: numbering.detail };\n424:     }\n425:     case 'file': {\n426:       const target = path.join(context.root, pin.path);\n427:       return fs.existsSync(target)\n428:         ? { state: PASS, detail: `${pin.path} exists` }\n429:         : { state: FINDING, detail: `${pin.path} is missing` };\n430:     }\n431:     default:\n432:       // A kind the engine has no rule for is a pin nobody measured. Counting it as a hold\n433:       // would report a manifest edit as verified.\n434:       return { state: UNKNOWN, detail: `no rule for a pin of kind ${pin.kind}` };\n435:   }\n436: }\n437: \n438: // A6. The delivery budget: the canary's own AGENTS.md lines, plus the two measurements\n439: // the report needs. Only this row's file is kept, because the canary also pins the skill\n440: // it ships in and a consumer legitimately has none of those.\n441: function rowBudget(context, manifest, tools) {\n442:   if (typeof tools.canary !== 'string' || !fs.existsSync(tools.canary)) {\n443:     return row('A6', 'delivery budget', UNKNOWN,\n444:       `the rule-copy canary is not installed at ${tools.canary || '(no path)'}; the budget cannot be measured`);\n445:   }\n446: \n447:   const run = spawnSync(process.execPath, [tools.canary, '--root', context.root], {\n448:     encoding: 'utf8',\n449:     timeout: TOOL_TIMEOUT_MS,\n450:     maxBuffer: 16 * 1024 * 1024,\n451:   });\n452:   const output = `${run.stdout || ''}\\n${run.stderr || ''}`.split('\\n').map((line) => line.trim()).filter((line) => line !== '');\n453:   if (run.error || (run.status !== 0 && run.status !== 1)) {\n454:     return row('A6', 'delivery budget', UNKNOWN,\n455:       `the rule-copy canary could not run: ${run.error ? run.error.message : `exit ${run.status}`}`);\n456:   }\n457: \n458:   // The canary prints its verdict, an anchor table and its findings. Only the findings\n459:   // that name this document belong to this row; its PASS and table lines are its own\n460:   // report, and the anchor end byte is measured below instead of parsed back out.\n461:   const own = output\n462:     .filter((line) => line.includes(manifest.root_file))\n463:     .filter((line) => /^(MISSING|BLOCKED):/u.test(line));\n464:   const elsewhere = output.filter((line) => !line.includes(manifest.root_file));\n465: \n466:   const limit = manifest.budget.prefix_bytes;\n467:   const ceiling = manifest.budget.max_bytes;\n468:   const prefixEnd = (manifest.pins || [])\n469:     .filter((pin) => pin.kind === 'delivery-anchor')\n470:     .reduce((max, pin) => {\n471:       const section = context.matched.find((entry) => entry.entry.id === pin.section);\n472:       if (!section) return max;\n473:       const end = deliveryAnchorEnd(section.section, pin);\n474:       return end === null ? max : Math.max(max, end);\n475:     }, 0);\n476: \n477:   const failures = [...own];\n478:   // The canary already names whatever it measured, so this row adds a figure only for a\n479:   // defect the canary did not report, instead of printing the same ceiling twice.\n480:   if (prefixEnd > limit && !own.some((line) => line.includes('delivery prefix'))) {\n481:     failures.push(`the last anchor ends at byte ${prefixEnd}, past the ${limit}-byte delivery prefix`);\n482:   }\n483:   if (context.totalBytes > ceiling && !own.some((line) => line.includes('ceiling'))) {\n484:     failures.push(`${context.totalBytes} bytes exceeds the ${ceiling}-byte ceiling`);\n485:   }\n486:   // A failing canary that names other files only is still a fact worth stating, because\n487:   // its exit status would otherwise read as this row's verdict. A passing one needs no\n488:   // note: its remaining lines are its own anchor table.\n489:   const notes = failures.length === 0 && run.status !== 0 && elsewhere.length > 0\n490:     ? [`the canary exits ${run.status} over ${elsewhere.length} line(s) outside ${manifest.root_file}, which belong to other files' pins`]\n491:     : [];\n492: \n493:   return row('A6', 'delivery budget', failures.length === 0 ? PASS : FINDING,\n494:     `${prefixEnd} of ${limit} bytes to the last anchor, ${context.totalBytes} of ${ceiling} bytes total`,\n495:     { lines: [...failures, ...notes] });\n496: }\n497: \n498: // A7. The repository block: one start marker line, one end marker line, in that order,\n499: // after every shipped section, small enough to leave headroom. A marker is the whole line,\n500: // so the same bytes quoted inside a sentence are prose and do not make a second block.\n501: function rowBlock(context, manifest) {\n502:   const { budget } = manifest;\n503:   const block = context.block;\n504:   const starts = block === null ? 0 : block.markerLines.start.length;\n505:   const ends = block === null ? 0 : block.markerLines.end.length;\n506: \n507:   if (starts === 0 && ends === 0) {\n508:     return row('A7', 'repo-local block', PASS, `no block; ${context.totalBytes} of ${budget.max_bytes} bytes used`);\n509:   }\n510:   if (starts !== 1 || ends !== 1) {\n511:     const lines = ['the block cannot be placed, so nothing inside it can be carried'];\n512:     const where = [];\n513:     if (block !== null && starts > 0) where.push(`the start marker sits on line ${block.markerLines.start.join(' and line ')}`);\n514:     if (block !== null && ends > 0) where.push(`the end marker on line ${block.markerLines.end.join(' and line ')}`);\n515:     if (where.length > 0) lines.push(where.join(', '));\n516:     return row('A7', 'repo-local block', FINDING,\n517:       `expected one start and one end marker line, found ${starts} and ${ends}`, { lines });\n518:   }\n519: \n520:   const lines = [];\n521:   if (!block || !block.complete) {\n522:     lines.push('the end marker sits before the start marker, so the block is out of order and unreadable');\n523:   }\n524:   // Whatever the document carries below the block is content a replacement has to place\n525:   // around the block, so a block above it cannot be the one block a rewrite writes last.\n526:   const following = block === null ? [] : context.sections.filter((section) => section.start > block.start);\n527:   if (block && block.complete && following.length > 0) {\n528:     lines.push(`the block starts at byte ${block.start}, before ${following.length} section(s) that follow it (the first is ${JSON.stringify(following[0].heading)} at byte ${following[0].start})`);\n529:   }\n530:   if (block && block.complete && block.start < budget.prefix_bytes) {\n531:     lines.push(`the block starts at byte ${block.start}, inside the ${budget.prefix_bytes}-byte delivery prefix`);\n532:   }\n533:   if (context.totalBytes > budget.max_bytes) {\n534:     lines.push(`${context.totalBytes} bytes exceeds the ${budget.max_bytes}-byte ceiling`);\n535:   }\n536: \n537:   const size = block && block.complete ? Buffer.byteLength(block.content, 'utf8') : 0;\n538:   const headroom = budget.max_bytes - context.totalBytes;\n539:   const detail = block && block.complete\n540:     ? `block ${size} bytes, ${headroom} bytes of headroom`\n541:     : `block unreadable, ${headroom} bytes of headroom`;\n542:   return row('A7', 'repo-local block', lines.length === 0 ? PASS : FINDING, detail,\n543:     { lines, entries: [{ size, headroom, complete: Boolean(block && block.complete) }] });\n544: }\n545: \n546: // A8. Repository content outside the block that matches no shipped section. This is the\n547: // content a replacement would drop, so it is reported by line and byte range. The text\n548: // above the first heading has no heading to match, and arrives here with a null one.\n549: function rowUnmarked(context, manifest) {\n550:   const prefixBytes = manifest.budget.prefix_bytes;\n551:   const entries = context.unregistered.map((entry) => ({\n552:     heading: entry.heading,\n553:     line_start: lineNumberAt(context.text, entry.start),\n554:     line_end: lineNumberAt(context.text, Math.max(entry.start, entry.end - 1)),\n555:     byte_start: entry.start,\n556:     byte_end: entry.end,\n557:     in_prefix: entry.start < prefixBytes,\n558:   }));\n559:   if (entries.length === 0) return row('A8', 'unmarked repo-unique content', PASS, 'no section outside the block');\n560: \n561:   const headings = entries.filter((entry) => entry.heading !== null).length;\n562:   const detail = [];\n563:   if (headings > 0) detail.push(`${headings} section(s) outside the block match no shipped section`);\n564:   if (entries.length > headings) detail.push('the text above the first heading is repository content no section carries');\n565: \n566:   return row('A8', 'unmarked repo-unique content', FINDING,\n567:     detail.join('; '),\n568:     {\n569:       lines: entries.map((entry) => `${entry.heading === null ? 'text before the first heading' : JSON.stringify(entry.heading)}: lines ${entry.line_start}-${entry.line_end}, bytes ${entry.byte_start}-${entry.byte_end}${entry.in_prefix ? ', inside the delivery prefix' : ''}`),\n570:       entries,\n571:     });\n572: }\n573: \n574: // The line where two bodies first part company, with the two raw lines. A body can carry\n575: // every line the shipped body carries and still not be it — an extra space on an indent, a\n576: // line written twice, two lines swapped — and the operator needs the line to look at\n577: // rather than a finding with nothing under it.\n578: function bodyDifference(actual, expected) {\n579:   const left = String(actual).split('\\n');\n580:   const right = String(expected).split('\\n');\n581:   const last = Math.max(left.length, right.length);\n582:   for (let index = 0; index < last; index += 1) {\n583:     if (left[index] === right[index]) continue;\n584:     return {\n585:       line: index + 1,\n586:       actual: left[index] === undefined ? null : left[index],\n587:       shipped: right[index] === undefined ? null : right[index],\n588:     };\n589:   }\n590:   return null;\n591: }\n592: \n593: // A9. For a section that is not the shipped body, the lines the shipped body does not\n594: // carry, once per occurrence. A body that carries every shipped line can still differ, so\n595: // the row names that difference by line instead of reporting a finding with no line under\n596: // it: a finding nobody can act on reads like a bug in the checker.\n597: function rowUnmarkedEdits(context, manifest) {\n598:   const entries = [];\n599:   for (const entry of context.currency) {\n600:     if (entry.state !== 'edited' && entry.state !== 'unknown-base') continue;\n601:     const shipped = manifest.derived.sections[entry.id];\n602:     const body = shipped ? shipped.body : '';\n603:     const lines = localLinesOf(entry.section.body, body);\n604:     entries.push({\n605:       id: entry.id,\n606:       state: entry.state,\n607:       lines,\n608:       difference: lines.length === 0 ? bodyDifference(entry.section.body, body) : null,\n609:     });\n610:   }\n611:   if (entries.length === 0) return row('A9', 'unmarked local edits', PASS, 'no edited section');\n612: \n613:   const total = entries.reduce((sum, entry) => sum + entry.lines.length, 0);\n614:   const formatting = entries.filter((entry) => entry.lines.length === 0).length;\n615:   const detail = [`${entries.length} section(s) carry ${total} local line(s)`];\n616:   if (formatting > 0) detail.push(`${formatting} of them differ only in whitespace, order or a repeat`);\n617: \n618:   const lines = [];\n619:   for (const entry of entries) {\n620:     for (const line of entry.lines) lines.push(`${entry.id}: ${line}`);\n621:     if (entry.difference === null) continue;\n622:     lines.push(`${entry.id}: only whitespace, order or a repeat differs from the shipped body, at line ${entry.difference.line}: shipped ${JSON.stringify(entry.difference.shipped)}, the document carries ${JSON.stringify(entry.difference.actual)}`);\n623:   }\n624:   return row('A9', 'unmarked local edits', lines.length === 0 ? INFO : FINDING,\n625:     detail.join('; '), { lines, entries });\n626: }\n627: \n628: // A10. The copies each runtime loads. Claude Code reaches the root document only through\n629: // CLAUDE.md, so a missing pointer leaves Claude without it and a full copy double-loads\n630: // and drifts. The Cursor rule is generated and alone carries the gate 1 lookup to Cursor\n631: // and Devin, so it is checked by its own generator.\n632: function rowRuntimeCopies(context, manifest, tools) {\n633:   const entries = [];\n634:   const lines = [];\n635: \n636:   for (const expected of manifest.runtime_copies || []) {\n637:     const verdict = copyVerdict(expected, context, manifest, tools);\n638:     entries.push({ path: expected.path, kind: verdict.kind, state: verdict.state, lines: verdict.lines || [] });\n639:     // A copy nobody could measure is printed like a finding: a pass line with no detail\n640:     // reads as a copy that was checked and found right.\n641:     if (verdict.state === FINDING || verdict.state === UNKNOWN || verdict.kind === 'pointer missing') {\n642:       lines.push(`${expected.path}: ${verdict.kind} - ${verdict.detail}`);\n643:     }\n644:   }\n645: \n646:   const failed = entries.filter((entry) => entry.state === FINDING);\n647:   const unmeasured = entries.filter((entry) => entry.state === UNKNOWN);\n648:   const state = failed.length > 0 ? FINDING : (unmeasured.length > 0 ? UNKNOWN : PASS);\n649:   const detail = `${entries.length - failed.length - unmeasured.length}/${entries.length} copies as expected${unmeasured.length > 0 ? `, ${unmeasured.length} unmeasured` : ''}`;\n650:   return row('A10', 'runtime copies', state, detail, { lines, entries });\n651: }\n652: \n653: function copyVerdict(expected, context, manifest, tools) {\n654:   if (expected.report_only) {\n655:     return { state: INFO, kind: 'reported only', detail: 'outside the checklist this command rewrites' };\n656:   }\n657:   if (expected.path.startsWith('~')) {\n658:     return { state: INFO, kind: 'outside the repository', detail: 'reported, never read' };\n659:   }\n660: \n661:   const target = path.join(context.root, expected.path);\n662:   if (expected.path.endsWith('.md') && expected.expect.includes('gate1-block-in-sync')) {\n663:     if (typeof tools.pointerSync !== 'string' || !fs.existsSync(tools.pointerSync)) {\n664:       return { state: UNKNOWN, kind: 'gate1 block unmeasured', detail: `the pointer sync is not installed at ${tools.pointerSync || '(no path)'}` };\n665:     }\n666:     const run = spawnSync(process.execPath, [tools.pointerSync, '--check', '--root', context.root], {\n667:       encoding: 'utf8',\n668:       timeout: TOOL_TIMEOUT_MS,\n669:     });\n670:     const output = `${run.stdout || ''}\\n${run.stderr || ''}`.split('\\n').map((line) => line.trim()).filter((line) => line !== '');\n671:     if (run.status === 0) return { state: PASS, kind: 'gate1 block in sync', detail: 'the generator reports no drift' };\n672:     if (run.status === 1) return { state: FINDING, kind: 'gate1 block out of sync', detail: output.join('; ') };\n673:     return { state: UNKNOWN, kind: 'gate1 block unmeasured', detail: output.join('; ') || `the pointer sync exited ${run.status}` };\n674:   }\n675: \n676:   const exists = fs.existsSync(target) || isSymlink(target);\n677:   const expectsAbsent = expected.expect.length === 1 && expected.expect[0] === 'absent';\n678: \n679:   if (expected.path === '.cursor/rules/repo-local.md') {\n680:     if (context.blockContent) {\n681:       if (!exists) return { state: FINDING, kind: 'mirror missing', detail: 'the block carries content and nothing mirrors it' };\n682:       const mirror = fs.readFileSync(target, 'utf8');\n683:       const missing = [...trimmedLines(context.block.content)].filter((line) => !mirror.includes(line));\n684:       return missing.length === 0\n685:         ? { state: PASS, kind: 'mirror current', detail: 'the mirror carries the block' }\n686:         : { state: FINDING, kind: 'mirror behind', detail: `${missing.length} block line(s) are not in the mirror` };\n687:     }\n688:     return exists\n689:       ? { state: FINDING, kind: 'mirror of an empty block', detail: 'the block is empty and a mirror exists' }\n690:       : { state: PASS, kind: 'absent', detail: 'no block content to mirror' };\n691:   }\n692: \n693:   // Claude Code reaches the root document only through this file, so its absence is a route\n694:   // the runtime does not have rather than a copy that is wrong.\n695:   if (expected.path === 'CLAUDE.md' && !exists && context.exists) {\n696:     return {\n697:       state: INFO,\n698:       kind: 'pointer missing',\n699:       detail: 'Claude Code reads only CLAUDE.md, so without a one-line @' + manifest.root_file + ' pointer it never loads ' + manifest.root_file,\n700:     };\n701:   }\n702: \n703:   if (!exists) {\n704:     return expectsAbsent\n705:       ? { state: PASS, kind: 'absent', detail: 'nothing to load twice' }\n706:       : { state: PASS, kind: 'absent', detail: 'absent, which the checklist allows' };\n707:   }\n708: \n709:   if (isSymlink(target)) {\n710:     const link = fs.readlinkSync(target);\n711:     // A path the checklist expects to be absent is one no runtime should load, and a link\n712:     // there loads the root document under that name just as a copy would.\n713:     if (expectsAbsent) {\n714:       return { state: FINDING, kind: 'symlink where none belongs', detail: `links to ${link}, where the checklist expects no file` };\n715:     }\n716:     const pointsAtRoot = path.basename(link) === manifest.root_file;\n717:     if (pointsAtRoot) return { state: PASS, kind: 'symlink', detail: `links to ${link}` };\n718:     return { state: FINDING, kind: 'symlink elsewhere', detail: `links to ${link} instead of ${manifest.root_file}` };\n719:   }\n720: \n721:   const content = fs.readFileSync(target, 'utf8');\n722:   if (!context.exists) {\n723:     return { state: FINDING, kind: 'sole instruction file', detail: `there is no ${manifest.root_file} for it to point at` };\n724:   }\n725:   if (content === context.text) {\n726:     return { state: FINDING, kind: 'identical copy', detail: `byte-identical to ${manifest.root_file}, so the two will drift` };\n727:   }\n728: \n729:   const unique = localLinesOf(content, context.text);\n730:   const rootLines = trimmedLines(context.text);\n731:   const sharesShippedLine = [...trimmedLines(content)].some((line) => rootLines.has(line));\n732:   const namesRoot = [...trimmedLines(content)].some((line) => line.includes(manifest.root_file));\n733:   // A pointer is a file that carries no shipped line and names the root document. Anything\n734:   // else is either a copy or a copy plus local lines, and both drift: the second also\n735:   // loses text when it is replaced, so the lines only it carries are listed.\n736:   if (!sharesShippedLine && namesRoot) {\n737:     return { state: PASS, kind: 'pointer', detail: `every line names ${manifest.root_file}` };\n738:   }\n739:   return { state: FINDING, kind: 'divergent copy', detail: `${unique.length} line(s) only this file carries`, lines: unique };\n740: }\n741: \n742: function isSymlink(target) {\n743:   try {\n744:     return fs.lstatSync(target).isSymbolicLink();\n745:   } catch {\n746:     return false;\n747:   }\n748: }\n749: \n750: // A11. The router: present, carrying the rows the template ships, and passing its own\n751: // checker. The checker's summary line is carried through, never rebuilt.\n752: function rowRouter(context, manifest, tools) {\n753:   const routerPath = path.join(context.root, manifest.router_file);\n754:   if (!fs.existsSync(routerPath)) {\n755:     return row('A11', 'REPO RULES.md', FINDING, 'absent',\n756:       { lines: [`no ${manifest.router_file}: no repository rule can load`], entries: [{ path: manifest.router_file, present: false }] });\n757:   }\n758: \n759:   const text = fs.readFileSync(routerPath, 'utf8');\n760:   const shipped = (manifest.derived && manifest.derived.repo_rules) || { trigger_rows: [], index_rows: [] };\n761:   const lines = [];\n762:   const missing = [\n763:     ...missingRows(shipped.trigger_rows, parseTableRows(text, 'TRIGGER TABLE'), 'trigger'),\n764:     ...missingRows(shipped.index_rows, parseTableRows(text, 'INDEX'), 'index'),\n765:   ];\n766:   for (const entry of missing) lines.push(`missing ${entry.kind} row for ${entry.rule}`);\n767: \n768:   if (typeof tools.repoRules !== 'string' || !fs.existsSync(tools.repoRules)) {\n769:     return row('A11', 'REPO RULES.md', UNKNOWN, 'present; the repo-rules checker is not installed',\n770:       { lines: [...lines, `the checker is not installed at ${tools.repoRules || '(no path)'}`] });\n771:   }\n772: \n773:   const run = spawnSync(process.execPath, [tools.repoRules, '--root', context.root], {\n774:     encoding: 'utf8',\n775:     timeout: TOOL_TIMEOUT_MS,\n776:   });\n777:   const output = `${run.stdout || ''}\\n${run.stderr || ''}`.split('\\n').map((line) => line.trim()).filter((line) => line !== '');\n778:   const summary = output.find((line) => line.includes(REPO_RULES_TAG) && line.includes('RESULT:'));\n779: \n780:   if (run.status === 2 || !summary) {\n781:     return row('A11', 'REPO RULES.md', UNKNOWN, 'the repo-rules checker could not run',\n782:       { lines: [...lines, ...output.filter((line) => line.includes(REPO_RULES_TAG))] });\n783:   }\n784:   if (run.status !== 0) {\n785:     return row('A11', 'REPO RULES.md', FINDING, `${missing.length} shipped row(s) missing; the checker fails`,\n786:       { lines: [...lines, summary] });\n787:   }\n788:   return row('A11', 'REPO RULES.md', lines.length === 0 ? PASS : FINDING,\n789:     `${missing.length} shipped row(s) missing; ${summary}`, { lines: [...lines, summary] });\n790: }\n791: \n792: // The router's own table, read the same way the manifest's rows were derived. Comparing\n793: // shipped rows to the file is the only way to say which of them a consumer dropped.\n794: function parseTableRows(text, anchor) {\n795:   const lines = text.split('\\n');\n796:   const start = lines.findIndex((line) => line.startsWith('#') && line.includes(anchor));\n797:   if (start === -1) return [];\n798: \n799:   const rows = [];\n800:   let width = 0;\n801:   for (let index = start + 1; index < lines.length; index += 1) {\n802:     const line = lines[index].trim();\n803:     if (line.startsWith('#')) break;\n804:     if (!line.startsWith('|')) {\n805:       if (rows.length > 0 && line === '') break;\n806:       continue;\n807:     }\n808:     const cells = line.replace(/^\\|/u, '').replace(/\\|$/u, '').split('|').map((cell) => cell.trim());\n809:     if (cells.every((cell) => /^:?-+:?$/u.test(cell) || cell === '')) continue;\n810:     if (width === 0) {\n811:       width = cells.length;\n812:       continue;\n813:     }\n814:     if (cells.length !== width) continue;\n815:     rows.push(cells);\n816:   }\n817:   return rows;\n818: }\n819: \n820: function rulePathOf(cells) {\n821:   for (const cell of cells) {\n822:     const linked = /\\(([^)]+\\.md)\\)/u.exec(cell);\n823:     if (linked) return linked[1];\n824:     const bare = /`([^`]+\\.md)`/u.exec(cell);\n825:     if (bare) return bare[1];\n826:   }\n827:   return null;\n828: }\n829: \n830: function missingRows(shipped, present, kind) {\n831:   const found = new Set(present.map((cells) => rulePathOf(cells) || cells.join(' | ')));\n832:   const missing = [];\n833:   for (const cells of shipped) {\n834:     const rule = rulePathOf(cells) || cells.join(' | ');\n835:     if (!found.has(rule)) missing.push({ kind, rule });\n836:   }\n837:   return missing;\n838: }\n839: \n840: // A12. The source repository's own two promises: its block stays empty, and its derived\n841: // copy of the template is fresh. A consumer's block is its own, so this row reports INFO\n842: // there instead of telling it what to do with its own file.\n843: function rowTemplatePurity(context, manifest, tools, options) {\n844:   let source = { isSource: false, reason: `origin was not compared` };\n845:   try {\n846:     source = sourceStatus({ root: context.root, manifest });\n847:   } catch (error) {\n848:     source = { isSource: false, reason: error.message };\n849:   }\n850: \n851:   if (!options.source && !source.isSource) {\n852:     return row('A12', 'template purity', INFO,\n853:       `runs only in the source repository; ${source.reason || 'the origin differs'}`);\n854:   }\n855: \n856:   const lines = [];\n857:   if (context.blockContent) lines.push('the source repository block carries content, and every line here ships to every consumer');\n858:   if (context.block && !context.block.complete) lines.push('the source repository block is not well formed');\n859: \n860:   if (typeof tools.generator !== 'string' || !fs.existsSync(tools.generator)) {\n861:     return row('A12', 'template purity', UNKNOWN,\n862:       `the manifest generator is not installed at ${tools.generator || '(no path)'}`);\n863:   }\n864:   const run = spawnSync(process.execPath, [tools.generator, '--check', '--root', context.root, '--manifest', options.manifest], {\n865:     encoding: 'utf8',\n866:     timeout: TOOL_TIMEOUT_MS,\n867:   });\n868:   const output = `${run.stdout || ''}\\n${run.stderr || ''}`.split('\\n').map((line) => line.trim()).filter((line) => line !== '');\n869:   if (run.status !== 0) lines.push(...output);\n870: \n871:   const state = lines.length === 0 ? PASS : FINDING;\n872:   return row('A12', 'template purity', state,\n873:     state === PASS ? 'block empty and the derived copy is fresh' : 'the source template is not pure', { lines });\n874: }\n875: \n876: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n877: // 6. REPORT\n878: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n879: \n880: function defaultTools(manifest) {\n881:   const canaryPin = (manifest.pins || []).find((pin) => String(pin.consumer || '').endsWith('check-rule-copies.js'));\n882:   const pointerPin = (manifest.pins || []).find((pin) => pin.kind === 'first-occurrence');\n883:   return {\n884:     canary: canaryPin ? path.join(REPO_ROOT, canaryPin.consumer) : null,\n885:     pointerSync: pointerPin ? path.join(REPO_ROOT, pointerPin.consumer) : null,\n886:     repoRules: REPO_RULES_CHECKER,\n887:     generator: GENERATOR,\n888:   };\n889: }\n890: \n891: /**\n892:  * Classify one repository.\n893:  *\n894:  * @param {{root?: string, manifestPath?: string, source?: boolean, tools?: Object}} [input]\n895:  * @returns {{root: string, manifest: string, state: string, status: string, exitCode: number,\n896:  *   findings: number, unknown: number, rows: Array<Object>} | null} Null when the root\n897:  *   directory or the manifest is not readable.\n898:  */\n899: function buildReport(input = {}) {\n900:   const root = input.root ? path.resolve(input.root) : REPO_ROOT;\n901:   const manifestPath = input.manifestPath ? path.resolve(input.manifestPath) : MANIFEST_PATH;\n902:   if (!fs.existsSync(manifestPath)) return null;\n903: \n904:   let manifest;\n905:   try {\n906:     manifest = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(manifestPath, 'utf8'));\n907:   } catch {\n908:     return null;\n909:   }\n910: \n911:   const context = readTarget({ root, manifest });\n912:   if (context === null) return null;\n913: \n914:   const tools = { ...defaultTools(manifest), ...(input.tools || {}) };\n915:   const rows = [\n916:     rowInstallState(context, manifest),\n917:     rowSectionsPresent(context, manifest),\n918:     rowOrderAndNumbering(context, manifest),\n919:     rowCurrency(context),\n920:     rowPins(context, manifest),\n921:     rowBudget(context, manifest, tools),\n922:     rowBlock(context, manifest),\n923:     rowUnmarked(context, manifest),\n924:     rowUnmarkedEdits(context, manifest),\n925:     rowRuntimeCopies(context, manifest, tools),\n926:     rowRouter(context, manifest, tools),\n927:     rowTemplatePurity(context, manifest, tools, { source: Boolean(input.source), manifest: manifestPath }),\n928:   ];\n929: \n930:   const findings = rows.filter((entry) => entry.state === FINDING).length;\n931:   const unknown = rows.filter((entry) => entry.state === UNKNOWN).length;\n932:   const exitCode = findings > 0 || unknown > 0 ? 1 : 0;\n933:   const status = exitCode === 0 ? 'OK' : (findings > 0 ? 'FINDINGS' : 'UNKNOWN');\n934: \n935:   return {\n936:     root,\n937:     manifest: manifestPath,\n938:     state: rows[0].detail,\n939:     status,\n940:     exitCode,\n941:     findings,\n942:     unknown,\n943:     rows,\n944:   };\n945: }\n946: \n947: // A long row is a list, not a wall: the report keeps every line in the JSON form and\n948: // prints the first few here, so a reader sees the shape before the volume.\n949: const PRINTED_LINES = 20;\n950: \n951: function renderText(report) {\n952:   const lines = [];\n953:   for (const entry of report.rows) {\n954:     lines.push(`${entry.id} ${entry.name}: ${entry.detail}   (${entry.state})`);\n955:     for (const line of entry.lines.slice(0, PRINTED_LINES)) lines.push(`  ${line}`);\n956:     if (entry.lines.length > PRINTED_LINES) {\n957:       lines.push(`  ... ${entry.lines.length - PRINTED_LINES} more line(s), all of them in the JSON report`);\n958:     }\n959:   }\n960:   lines.push(`STATUS=${report.status} rows=${report.rows.length} findings=${report.findings} unknown=${report.unknown}`);\n961:   return `${lines.join('\\n')}\\n`;\n962: }\n963: \n964: function renderJson(report) {\n965:   const shaped = {\n966:     root: report.root,\n967:     manifest: report.manifest,\n968:     state: report.state,\n969:     status: report.status,\n970:     exit_code: report.exitCode,\n971:     findings: report.findings,\n972:     unknown: report.unknown,\n973:     rows: report.rows.map((entry) => ({\n974:       id: entry.id,\n975:       name: entry.name,\n976:       state: entry.state,\n977:       detail: entry.detail,\n978:       lines: entry.lines,\n979:       entries: entry.entries,\n980:     })),\n981:   };\n982:   return `${JSON.stringify(shaped, null, 2)}\\n`;\n983: }\n984: \n985: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n986: // 7. MAIN\n987: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n988: \n989: /**\n990:  * @param {string[]} argv\n991:  * @param {{tools?: Object}} [hooks] - test seams for the child tools\n992:  * @returns {number} the process exit code\n993:  */\n994: function main(argv, hooks = {}) {\n995:   const options = parseArgs(argv);\n996:   if (options.help) {\n997:     console.log(USAGE);\n998:     return 0;\n999:   }\n1000: \n1001:   if (!fs.existsSync(options.manifest)) {\n1002:     console.error(`${TAG} ERROR: the manifest is missing at ${options.manifest}`);\n1003:     return 2;\n1004:   }\n1005:   if (!fs.existsSync(options.root) || !fs.statSync(options.root).isDirectory()) {\n1006:     console.error(`${TAG} ERROR: the target root is not a directory: ${options.root}`);\n1007:     return 2;\n1008:   }\n1009:   // The root file's name is the manifest's to declare, so this precondition reads the same\n1010:   // declaration every other read does instead of a name of its own.\n1011:   let rootFile = null;\n1012:   try {\n1013:     rootFile = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(options.manifest, 'utf8')).root_file || null;\n1014:   } catch {\n1015:     // An unreadable manifest is refused below, where the run has no report to print.\n1016:     rootFile = null;\n1017:   }\n1018:   const rootPath = rootFile === null ? null : path.join(options.root, rootFile);\n1019:   if (rootPath !== null && fs.existsSync(rootPath)) {\n1020:     try {\n1021:       fs.readFileSync(rootPath, 'utf8');\n1022:     } catch (error) {\n1023:       console.error(`${TAG} ERROR: cannot read ${rootPath}: ${error.message}`);\n1024:       return 2;\n1025:     }\n1026:   }\n1027: \n1028:   const report = buildReport({\n1029:     root: options.root,\n1030:     manifestPath: options.manifest,\n1031:     source: options.source,\n1032:     tools: hooks.tools || {},\n1033:   });\n1034:   if (report === null) {\n1035:     // A manifest that cannot be parsed leaves nothing to compare against, and an empty\n1036:     // report would read like a clean one.\n1037:     console.error(`${TAG} ERROR: cannot classify ${options.root}: the manifest at ${options.manifest} could not be read as JSON`);\n1038:     return 2;\n1039:   }\n1040: \n1041:   process.stdout.write(options.json ? renderJson(report) : renderText(report));\n1042:   return report.exitCode;\n1043: }\n1044: \n1045: if (require.main === module) {\n1046:   try {\n1047:     process.exitCode = main(process.argv.slice(2));\n1048:   } catch (error) {\n1049:     console.error(`${TAG} ERROR: ${error.message}`);\n1050:     if (error instanceof UsageError) console.error(USAGE);\n1051:     process.exitCode = 2;\n1052:   }\n1053: }\n1054: \n1055: module.exports = {\n1056:   MANIFEST_PATH,\n1057:   buildReport,\n1058:   main,\n1059:   renderJson,\n1060:   renderText,\n1061: };"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\".skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-check.cjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794558774847,"end":1794558775175}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794558777299,"sessionID":"ses_e2bf6bc5bffePiQWy1Q3jiC9P1","part":{"id":"prt_1d409d7d3001gEdvfd0s6DY2OP","sessionID":"ses_e2bf6bc5bffePiQWy1Q3jiC9P1","messageID":"msg_1d409b985001HQ1kxYcJpDny6v","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"86601f2916bbca8b8966bf982f331886ee9b41b3","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":2634,"output":83,"reasoning":68,"cache":{"read":21632,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794558801559,"sessionID":"ses_e2bf6bc5bffePiQWy1Q3jiC9P1","part":{"id":"prt_1d40a36970014DctHC7xMsr6MG","sessionID":"ses_e2bf6bc5bffePiQWy1Q3jiC9P1","messageID":"msg_1d409d7de001knMGfxRrIRYLRq","type":"step-start","snapshot":"9203482c106a4fa7f8b76d4791969c0a0eb3bd2a"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794558802620,"sessionID":"ses_e2bf6bc5bffePiQWy1Q3jiC9P1","part":{"partID":"prt_1d40a384e001v97iPWkjsTzn30","sessionID":"ses_e2bf6bc5bffePiQWy1Q3jiC9P1","messageID":"msg_1d409d7de001knMGfxRrIRYLRq","type":"tool","id":"call_b484c0f5dd6544828b1c038c","tool":"skill","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"id":"sk-code"},"output":"<skill_content name=\"sk-code\">\n# Skill: sk-code\n\n<!-- Keywords: sk-code, code, surface-aware, two-axis, opencode, typescript, python, shell, json, jsonc, github-actions, ci-workflow, sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend, quality, code-review, verification, debug, implement, workflow-doctrine, mode-registry, workflowmode, packetkind, backendkind, surface-router, evidence-base, surface-packet, guard, adapter, stderr, detector -->\n\n# Code Family Hub (sk-code)\n\nOne skill has two axes, one shared surface-detection router and one advisor identity.\nThe **workflow axis** has two code modes that act: sk-code-quality and sk-code-review.\nThe **surface axis** has three read-only evidence packets the hub bundles alongside a workflow mode: sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend and sk-code-skilled.\nEach packet carries the shared **implement → debug → verify** workflow doctrine (`shared/references/workflow-*.md`, symlinked in) plus its own domain knowledge.\nThis hub holds NO per-mode logic. It routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each mode or surface keeps its own contract in its packet.\n\n**Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`.\n`description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value. Each hub-root JSON routing file therefore states the same release.\n`ROUTER.md` and `README.md` keep their own document versions, as the frontmatter-versioning standard gives every doc but `SKILL.md`.\nThe authority stops at the hub root. Each `modes[]` packet carries its own version and release cadence.\nA packet version is independent of this one by design, so a gap between them is not drift.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill for code-family workflows. Invoke it as `sk-code` with an optional mode hint such as `quality: <request>`. The hub classifies the request, resolves a mode key, and loads the matching nested packet.\n\n**Workflow axis** (modes that act, one of them chosen as primary):\n\n| Mode | Use it for | Packet |\n|------|------------|--------|\n| **sk-code-quality** | Quality gate, P0/P1/P2 author checks, comment hygiene and surface checklists. | `sk-code/sk-code-quality/` |\n| **sk-code-review** | Findings-first review, security and correctness baseline, checklists, output contract and PR-state gates. | `sk-code/sk-code-review/` |\n\n**Surface axis** (read-only, advisor-invisible evidence. When a workflow mode scores, the hub bundles the surface after that primary mode. When no workflow mode scores, the hub routes the surface alone for a lookup, implement, debug or verify task, and the acting agent hands the author gates to `sk-code-quality` before it claims completion):\n\n| Surface | Carries | Packet |\n|---------|---------|--------|\n| **sk-code-ci** | GitHub Actions evidence: trigger, filter, permission, timeout, concurrency and caching rules, the protected set, the intentional message-contract exception and the scaffold templates, with the repository-specific values read from a CI profile. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-ci/` |\n| **sk-code-frontend** | Stack-agnostic frontend evidence: web markup and CSS, web layout and accessibility, visual values, visual hierarchy and review, motion and interaction, the `tokens.css` starter tokens and Figma-to-code checks. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-frontend/` |\n| **sk-code-skilled** | System-code evidence: TypeScript/Python/shell/config standards, hooks, alignment verification, authoring checklists. Bundles the implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine (read-only evidence) for the Skilled surface, and the acting agent applies it. | `sk-code/sk-code-skilled/` |\n\n**Surface list.** The hub has exactly three surfaces:\n- SKILLED (`sk-code-skilled`): the native surface for this framework's own code.\n- CI (`sk-code-ci`): the GitHub Actions workflow standards surface.\n- Frontend (`sk-code-frontend`): the stack-agnostic web practice and Figma-to-code surface, with visual values, visual hierarchy and review, and motion and interaction guidance, bundled from request vocabulary alone.\n\nThe hub also has two workflow modes: quality (`sk-code-quality`) and review (`sk-code-review`).\nAny other sk-code doc that counts surfaces points to this sentence.\n\n### When NOT to Use\n- Documentation-only changes with no code-work contract - use `sk-doc`.\n- Git workflow, branch, commit, PR, merge, or release work - use `sk-git`.\n- Pure browser inspection or external MCP transport work - use the relevant MCP skill.\n- For a measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source), use `sk-design-md-generator` first.\n- When a request or a frame leaves a visual value open (spacing, type, color, radius, shadow or motion), or a value sits off every token and scale, load `sk-code-frontend` and decide it from its visual-values reference.\n- Backend support primitives in `shared/` - they are consumed by the modes, not invoked as a user workflow.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nRouting is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\nStage 1 (hub → mode) uses `hub-router.json` as the authored mode vocabulary. `mode-registry.json` carries the packet contracts, whose aliases are checked against that vocabulary.\nStage 2 (mode → leaves) uses the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) as the authored leaf-set router.\nBoth files point back here instead of restating the split.\n\nThe advisor routes every code query to the single identity `sk-code`. Resolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"` and follow the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\nCompiled routing is the default. The legacy fallback, the kill-switch and the `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST` live in the compiled-routing section of [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md).\n\nA bare implement, debug or verify request with no workflow mode becomes a pure surface bundle, and an unclear code intent asks for disambiguation. Neither path forces a stale default.\nRun the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Terms\n\n- **`workflowMode`**: the public mode or surface key a request resolves to.\n- **`packetKind`**: the axis a packet sits on, `workflow` for a mode that acts and `surface` for read-only evidence bundled alongside a mode.\n- **`backendKind`**: which backend runs the packet, `surface-router` or `review-cache` for a workflow mode and `evidence-base` for a surface.\n- **`surfaceBundle`**: the `routerPolicy.outcomes` entry where one workflow mode leads and zero or more surfaces follow as evidence.\n- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each mode's discriminator, `surfaceDetection` markers and `advisorRouting`.\n- **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's `routerSignals`, `vocabularyClasses` and the surface-bundling policy.\n- **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, plus the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list and the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble.\n- **shared control**: a hub-level resource declared in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, which resolves on disk and never projects as a leaf.\n- **leaf**: a typed `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n- **surface detection**: the shared detection order over the declared surfaces, which tries each specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n- **`--surface-hint`**: the front-door flag that carries the detected surface into the compiled route, so a hinted surface leads a keyword tie.\n- **`UNKNOWN_FALLBACK`**: the load level the router returns, with a disambiguation checklist, when no mode resolves.\n\n### Layout\n```\nsk-code/\n  SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-mode code logic)\n  README.md              # human-facing front page for the hub\n  ROUTER.md              # active stage-two surface router and shared-control declaration\n  mode-registry.json     # the two-axis discriminator + advisorRouting contract\n  hub-router.json        # lexical routing signals + surfaceBundle policy for hub-local choice\n  description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n  graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n  leaf-manifest.json     # typed (workflowMode, leaf) pairs the router guards validate\n  changelog/             # hub release notes, one v<version>.md per hub release\n  feature-catalog/       # current-state inventory of the hub's routing capabilities\n  manual-testing-playbook/  # routing and disambiguation scenarios for the hub\n  benchmark/             # historical routing benchmark inputs and reports\n  sk-code-quality/       # quality mode packet     (workflow)\n  sk-code-review/        # review mode packet      (workflow)\n  sk-code-ci/            # GitHub Actions workflow standards packet (read-only evidence; rules, protected set and scaffold templates)\n  sk-code-frontend/      # stack-agnostic web and Figma-to-code evidence packet (read-only; references/web-visual-values.md, web-visual-hierarchy.md and web-motion-and-interaction.md plus assets/tokens.css)\n  sk-code-skilled/      # skilled surface packet (read-only evidence; carries the workflow doctrine)\n  shared/                # shared surface-detection router, cross-mode helpers, and the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine (references/workflow-*.md)\n```\n\nEach mode or surface packet is self-contained and carries no per-packet `graph-metadata.json`. Only this hub carries one, so the advisor discovers exactly one code skill identity. The implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine is a single shared source under `shared/references/`, and each surface symlinks it in rather than duplicating it.\n\n### Backend\nThe `surface-router` backend is the shared surface-detection router under `shared/`.\nIt centralizes the detection order over the declared surfaces, the SKILLED fallback and UNKNOWN.\nIt carries the shared implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine consumed by each surface.\nThe `review-cache` backend supports the sk-code-review mode's non-mutating review output cache.\nThe `evidence-base` backend serves the surface packets as read-only domain evidence (`sk-code-ci/`, `sk-code-frontend/`, `sk-code-skilled/`). The hub bundles them alongside the primary mode.\nThe packet mutates nothing and never carries process. The acting agent applies the doctrine it bundles.\nShared backend material provides surface identity, the workflow doctrine and cross-mode helpers.\nIt must never gain per-mode workflow contracts.\n\nThe **implement → debug → verify** phases are not standalone modes.\nTheir surface-agnostic doctrine lives once in `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `workflow-debug.md` and `workflow-verify.md`.\nThose files are symlinked into each surface, so the active surface carries the full workflow.\nA request to implement, debug or verify code causes the router to detect its surface and load that surface's bundled doctrine.\nThe acting agent applies that doctrine.\n\n### Maintenance\n\nAfter adding or removing a surface, run `/doctor:sk-code setup`. It checks the hub and names the repair for each failing check.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RULES\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n- **ALWAYS** resolve a mode through `mode-registry.json`, and never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n- **ALWAYS** keep mode contracts in the mode packets, so the hub stays routing-only.\n- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one skill identity.\n- **ALWAYS** give every mode an `advisorRouting` block with `routingClass: \"metadata\"` and the correct `packetSkillName`.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine as one shared source under `shared/references/`, symlinked into each surface, and never fork per-surface copies.\n- **ALWAYS** keep `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` keys aligned with its intent keys and packet leaves aligned with `leaf-manifest.json`.\n- **ALWAYS** keep hub-shared map inputs explicit in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, because they are control resources and never typed leaves.\n\n### ⛔ NEVER\n- **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a mode packet or `shared/`.\n- **NEVER** put per-mode quality, review, or surface workflow logic in the hub.\n- **NEVER** hardcode a router mapping outside `mode-registry.json`.\n- **NEVER** reintroduce implement, debug, or verify as standalone mode packets, because they are surface-owned doctrine.\n\n### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n- A new code mode is needed - extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet. Do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n- Surface identity and workflow ownership conflict in a way the shared router cannot classify.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n- Workflow mode packets: `sk-code-quality/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-review/SKILL.md`.\n- Surface evidence packets: `sk-code-ci/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-frontend/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md`.\n- Shared workflow doctrine: `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `shared/references/workflow-debug.md`, `shared/references/workflow-verify.md` (symlinked into each surface).\n- Registry: `mode-registry.json` (two-axis: `packetKind` discriminates workflow vs surface).\n- Hub router signals + surface bundling: `hub-router.json`.\n- Surface router and shared controls: `ROUTER.md`.\n- Parent-skill pattern: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n- Sibling example: `.skilled/skills/sk-prompt/`.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/failed-runs.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/findings-and-recommendations.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/skill-benchmark-report.json</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/skill-benchmark-report.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/source.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>","title":"skill","metadata":{"metadata":{"name":"sk-code","directory":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code","truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"<skill_content name=\"sk-code\">\n# Skill: sk-code\n\n<!-- Keywords: sk-code, code, surface-aware, two-axis, opencode, typescript, python, shell, json, jsonc, github-actions, ci-workflow, sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend, quality, code-review, verification, debug, implement, workflow-doctrine, mode-registry, workflowmode, packetkind, backendkind, surface-router, evidence-base, surface-packet, guard, adapter, stderr, detector -->\n\n# Code Family Hub (sk-code)\n\nOne skill has two axes, one shared surface-detection router and one advisor identity.\nThe **workflow axis** has two code modes that act: sk-code-quality and sk-code-review.\nThe **surface axis** has three read-only evidence packets the hub bundles alongside a workflow mode: sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend and sk-code-skilled.\nEach packet carries the shared **implement → debug → verify** workflow doctrine (`shared/references/workflow-*.md`, symlinked in) plus its own domain knowledge.\nThis hub holds NO per-mode logic. It routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each mode or surface keeps its own contract in its packet.\n\n**Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`.\n`description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value. Each hub-root JSON routing file therefore states the same release.\n`ROUTER.md` and `README.md` keep their own document versions, as the frontmatter-versioning standard gives every doc but `SKILL.md`.\nThe authority stops at the hub root. Each `modes[]` packet carries its own version and release cadence.\nA packet version is independent of this one by design, so a gap between them is not drift.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill for code-family workflows. Invoke it as `sk-code` with an optional mode hint such as `quality: <request>`. The hub classifies the request, resolves a mode key, and loads the matching nested packet.\n\n**Workflow axis** (modes that act, one of them chosen as primary):\n\n| Mode | Use it for | Packet |\n|------|------------|--------|\n| **sk-code-quality** | Quality gate, P0/P1/P2 author checks, comment hygiene and surface checklists. | `sk-code/sk-code-quality/` |\n| **sk-code-review** | Findings-first review, security and correctness baseline, checklists, output contract and PR-state gates. | `sk-code/sk-code-review/` |\n\n**Surface axis** (read-only, advisor-invisible evidence. When a workflow mode scores, the hub bundles the surface after that primary mode. When no workflow mode scores, the hub routes the surface alone for a lookup, implement, debug or verify task, and the acting agent hands the author gates to `sk-code-quality` before it claims completion):\n\n| Surface | Carries | Packet |\n|---------|---------|--------|\n| **sk-code-ci** | GitHub Actions evidence: trigger, filter, permission, timeout, concurrency and caching rules, the protected set, the intentional message-contract exception and the scaffold templates, with the repository-specific values read from a CI profile. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-ci/` |\n| **sk-code-frontend** | Stack-agnostic frontend evidence: web markup and CSS, web layout and accessibility, visual values, visual hierarchy and review, motion and interaction, the `tokens.css` starter tokens and Figma-to-code checks. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-frontend/` |\n| **sk-code-skilled** | System-code evidence: TypeScript/Python/shell/config standards, hooks, alignment verification, authoring checklists. Bundles the implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine (read-only evidence) for the Skilled surface, and the acting agent applies it. | `sk-code/sk-code-skilled/` |\n\n**Surface list.** The hub has exactly three surfaces:\n- SKILLED (`sk-code-skilled`): the native surface for this framework's own code.\n- CI (`sk-code-ci`): the GitHub Actions workflow standards surface.\n- Frontend (`sk-code-frontend`): the stack-agnostic web practice and Figma-to-code surface, with visual values, visual hierarchy and review, and motion and interaction guidance, bundled from request vocabulary alone.\n\nThe hub also has two workflow modes: quality (`sk-code-quality`) and review (`sk-code-review`).\nAny other sk-code doc that counts surfaces points to this sentence.\n\n### When NOT to Use\n- Documentation-only changes with no code-work contract - use `sk-doc`.\n- Git workflow, branch, commit, PR, merge, or release work - use `sk-git`.\n- Pure browser inspection or external MCP transport work - use the relevant MCP skill.\n- For a measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source), use `sk-design-md-generator` first.\n- When a request or a frame leaves a visual value open (spacing, type, color, radius, shadow or motion), or a value sits off every token and scale, load `sk-code-frontend` and decide it from its visual-values reference.\n- Backend support primitives in `shared/` - they are consumed by the modes, not invoked as a user workflow.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nRouting is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\nStage 1 (hub → mode) uses `hub-router.json` as the authored mode vocabulary. `mode-registry.json` carries the packet contracts, whose aliases are checked against that vocabulary.\nStage 2 (mode → leaves) uses the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) as the authored leaf-set router.\nBoth files point back here instead of restating the split.\n\nThe advisor routes every code query to the single identity `sk-code`. Resolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"` and follow the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\nCompiled routing is the default. The legacy fallback, the kill-switch and the `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST` live in the compiled-routing section of [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md).\n\nA bare implement, debug or verify request with no workflow mode becomes a pure surface bundle, and an unclear code intent asks for disambiguation. Neither path forces a stale default.\nRun the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Terms\n\n- **`workflowMode`**: the public mode or surface key a request resolves to.\n- **`packetKind`**: the axis a packet sits on, `workflow` for a mode that acts and `surface` for read-only evidence bundled alongside a mode.\n- **`backendKind`**: which backend runs the packet, `surface-router` or `review-cache` for a workflow mode and `evidence-base` for a surface.\n- **`surfaceBundle`**: the `routerPolicy.outcomes` entry where one workflow mode leads and zero or more surfaces follow as evidence.\n- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each mode's discriminator, `surfaceDetection` markers and `advisorRouting`.\n- **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's `routerSignals`, `vocabularyClasses` and the surface-bundling policy.\n- **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, plus the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list and the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble.\n- **shared control**: a hub-level resource declared in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, which resolves on disk and never projects as a leaf.\n- **leaf**: a typed `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n- **surface detection**: the shared detection order over the declared surfaces, which tries each specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n- **`--surface-hint`**: the front-door flag that carries the detected surface into the compiled route, so a hinted surface leads a keyword tie.\n- **`UNKNOWN_FALLBACK`**: the load level the router returns, with a disambiguation checklist, when no mode resolves.\n\n### Layout\n```\nsk-code/\n  SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-mode code logic)\n  README.md              # human-facing front page for the hub\n  ROUTER.md              # active stage-two surface router and shared-control declaration\n  mode-registry.json     # the two-axis discriminator + advisorRouting contract\n  hub-router.json        # lexical routing signals + surfaceBundle policy for hub-local choice\n  description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n  graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n  leaf-manifest.json     # typed (workflowMode, leaf) pairs the router guards validate\n  changelog/             # hub release notes, one v<version>.md per hub release\n  feature-catalog/       # current-state inventory of the hub's routing capabilities\n  manual-testing-playbook/  # routing and disambiguation scenarios for the hub\n  benchmark/             # historical routing benchmark inputs and reports\n  sk-code-quality/       # quality mode packet     (workflow)\n  sk-code-review/        # review mode packet      (workflow)\n  sk-code-ci/            # GitHub Actions workflow standards packet (read-only evidence; rules, protected set and scaffold templates)\n  sk-code-frontend/      # stack-agnostic web and Figma-to-code evidence packet (read-only; references/web-visual-values.md, web-visual-hierarchy.md and web-motion-and-interaction.md plus assets/tokens.css)\n  sk-code-skilled/      # skilled surface packet (read-only evidence; carries the workflow doctrine)\n  shared/                # shared surface-detection router, cross-mode helpers, and the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine (references/workflow-*.md)\n```\n\nEach mode or surface packet is self-contained and carries no per-packet `graph-metadata.json`. Only this hub carries one, so the advisor discovers exactly one code skill identity. The implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine is a single shared source under `shared/references/`, and each surface symlinks it in rather than duplicating it.\n\n### Backend\nThe `surface-router` backend is the shared surface-detection router under `shared/`.\nIt centralizes the detection order over the declared surfaces, the SKILLED fallback and UNKNOWN.\nIt carries the shared implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine consumed by each surface.\nThe `review-cache` backend supports the sk-code-review mode's non-mutating review output cache.\nThe `evidence-base` backend serves the surface packets as read-only domain evidence (`sk-code-ci/`, `sk-code-frontend/`, `sk-code-skilled/`). The hub bundles them alongside the primary mode.\nThe packet mutates nothing and never carries process. The acting agent applies the doctrine it bundles.\nShared backend material provides surface identity, the workflow doctrine and cross-mode helpers.\nIt must never gain per-mode workflow contracts.\n\nThe **implement → debug → verify** phases are not standalone modes.\nTheir surface-agnostic doctrine lives once in `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `workflow-debug.md` and `workflow-verify.md`.\nThose files are symlinked into each surface, so the active surface carries the full workflow.\nA request to implement, debug or verify code causes the router to detect its surface and load that surface's bundled doctrine.\nThe acting agent applies that doctrine.\n\n### Maintenance\n\nAfter adding or removing a surface, run `/doctor:sk-code setup`. It checks the hub and names the repair for each failing check.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RULES\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n- **ALWAYS** resolve a mode through `mode-registry.json`, and never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n- **ALWAYS** keep mode contracts in the mode packets, so the hub stays routing-only.\n- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one skill identity.\n- **ALWAYS** give every mode an `advisorRouting` block with `routingClass: \"metadata\"` and the correct `packetSkillName`.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine as one shared source under `shared/references/`, symlinked into each surface, and never fork per-surface copies.\n- **ALWAYS** keep `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` keys aligned with its intent keys and packet leaves aligned with `leaf-manifest.json`.\n- **ALWAYS** keep hub-shared map inputs explicit in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, because they are control resources and never typed leaves.\n\n### ⛔ NEVER\n- **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a mode packet or `shared/`.\n- **NEVER** put per-mode quality, review, or surface workflow logic in the hub.\n- **NEVER** hardcode a router mapping outside `mode-registry.json`.\n- **NEVER** reintroduce implement, debug, or verify as standalone mode packets, because they are surface-owned doctrine.\n\n### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n- A new code mode is needed - extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet. Do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n- Surface identity and workflow ownership conflict in a way the shared router cannot classify.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n- Workflow mode packets: `sk-code-quality/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-review/SKILL.md`.\n- Surface evidence packets: `sk-code-ci/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-frontend/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md`.\n- Shared workflow doctrine: `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `shared/references/workflow-debug.md`, `shared/references/workflow-verify.md` (symlinked into each surface).\n- Registry: `mode-registry.json` (two-axis: `packetKind` discriminates workflow vs surface).\n- Hub router signals + surface bundling: `hub-router.json`.\n- Surface router and shared controls: `ROUTER.md`.\n- Parent-skill pattern: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n- Sibling example: `.skilled/skills/sk-prompt/`.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/failed-runs.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/findings-and-recommendations.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/skill-benchmark-report.json</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/skill-benchmark-report.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/source.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"id\":\"sk-code\"}"},"time":{"start":1794558801998,"end":1794558802620}}}}
